WEBVTT

1
00:00:06,214 --> 00:00:09,175
[audience] <i>♪ Olé, olé, olé, olé ♪</i>

2
00:00:09,259 --> 00:00:11,720
[electric guitar intro playing]

3
00:00:13,430 --> 00:00:16,474
[Fito] What bothers me about documentaries

4
00:00:16,558 --> 00:00:18,643
is they lack any sense of malice.

5
00:00:22,689 --> 00:00:28,945
Whoever they're about always comes across
as some kind of saint.

6
00:00:29,738 --> 00:00:32,699
All kindness and good manners.

7
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:37,245
That really gets on my nerves.

8
00:00:37,746 --> 00:00:40,749
I need things to feel real.

9
00:00:41,249 --> 00:00:45,420
I need to see the real stuff, you know?
That's it.

10
00:00:45,503 --> 00:00:47,964
Of course, in life,
you gotta put on an act.

11
00:00:48,048 --> 00:00:52,635
If not, you couldn't survive.
We'd be at each other's throats.

12
00:00:53,762 --> 00:00:54,971
[clears throat]

13
00:00:55,055 --> 00:00:56,723
[urinating]

14
00:00:56,806 --> 00:00:58,975
But yeah, malice.

15
00:01:00,602 --> 00:01:04,272
-["Al lado del camino" playing]
-[audience cheering]

16
00:01:08,485 --> 00:01:10,153
[presenter] <i>Ladies and gentlemen,</i>

17
00:01:11,529 --> 00:01:13,490
<i>Fito Páez!</i>

18
00:01:13,573 --> 00:01:15,200
[crowd cheering]

19
00:01:23,583 --> 00:01:25,043
[Fito] I'm 24 years old.

20
00:01:26,044 --> 00:01:27,212
I was born in Rosario.

21
00:01:27,295 --> 00:01:30,882
I was thrown into this world.
I don't know what goes on here.

22
00:01:35,678 --> 00:01:37,889
Rosario was a bleak place in the '70s.

23
00:01:37,972 --> 00:01:41,434
There was nothing there,
so we had to invent our own world.

24
00:01:45,355 --> 00:01:48,108
The plan was to go to Buenos Aires
and record an album.

25
00:01:48,191 --> 00:01:50,401
We were going to make a living from music.

26
00:01:50,985 --> 00:01:52,904
It was amazing, dizzying.

27
00:01:55,073 --> 00:01:57,992
Music gives my life meaning.

28
00:01:58,076 --> 00:02:01,663
It could all go to hell.
It could all go off the rails in a second.

29
00:02:01,746 --> 00:02:04,541
But while it's here, this matters.

30
00:02:04,624 --> 00:02:07,335
Somebody has to do it. Luckily, that's me.

31
00:02:11,631 --> 00:02:15,176
I'd go out, and there'd be
50 girls going… [screams]

32
00:02:24,519 --> 00:02:27,647
There's a lot of pressure
to express yourself.

33
00:02:27,730 --> 00:02:30,900
It's like a pressure cooker.

34
00:02:30,984 --> 00:02:35,029
You don't even know
why you need to let it out.

35
00:02:35,113 --> 00:02:39,284
The only thing I can say
for sure is that it happened to me.

36
00:02:39,367 --> 00:02:40,869
It's still happening.

37
00:02:46,416 --> 00:02:53,173
THE WORLD WITHIN A SONG

38
00:02:56,342 --> 00:02:59,220
LONDON

39
00:02:59,304 --> 00:03:01,723
[classical music playing]

40
00:03:05,643 --> 00:03:10,023
[Eugenia] These ones?
Or these? They're kind of broken.

41
00:03:10,106 --> 00:03:12,859
-Not these ones.
-They've got sand on them.

42
00:03:12,942 --> 00:03:14,027
-Sand?
-I don't know.

43
00:03:14,110 --> 00:03:17,071
But I have to read, Eugenia.
Should I take these?

44
00:03:17,906 --> 00:03:20,116
-Bye.
-[Eugenia] Bye, honey. Good luck.

45
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:22,202
[Fito kisses] Mwah.

46
00:03:22,285 --> 00:03:23,578
Good luck.

47
00:03:24,704 --> 00:03:26,164
[Fito clears throat]

48
00:03:29,542 --> 00:03:32,003
[Fito] Music is my mother.

49
00:03:32,545 --> 00:03:34,839
It's where I can rest. My comfort zone.

50
00:03:34,923 --> 00:03:37,258
I float. I fly. I play.

51
00:03:37,342 --> 00:03:39,636
With other things, I have to think.

52
00:03:40,178 --> 00:03:43,014
-[in English] Hey, my friend! Thank you.
-Thank you.

53
00:03:44,474 --> 00:03:47,810
[man 1] They put the wrong…
It's a city, is it?

54
00:03:47,894 --> 00:03:50,980
Or the Abbey Road studios in Abbey Road?

55
00:03:51,064 --> 00:03:53,107
The… the… the studio.

56
00:03:53,942 --> 00:03:56,236
[man] No. It's wrong. That's why…

57
00:03:58,279 --> 00:04:00,114
What is the address?

58
00:04:01,950 --> 00:04:05,245
-Is it Abbey Road Studios in Abbey Road?
-[man 2] Yeah.

59
00:04:05,328 --> 00:04:06,537
[laughs]

60
00:04:06,621 --> 00:04:08,623
[in Spanish] Where the Beatles recorded.

61
00:04:08,706 --> 00:04:10,541
[in English] Where Beatles recorded.

62
00:04:10,625 --> 00:04:12,585
[church organ playing]

63
00:04:12,669 --> 00:04:16,631
[in Spanish] The Beatles invented youth.
Just think about that.

64
00:04:16,714 --> 00:04:20,009
{\an8}It's pure joy. Hormones. Wit…

65
00:04:20,093 --> 00:04:21,010
{\an8}RECORDING OF NOVELA

66
00:04:21,803 --> 00:04:25,848
{\an8}Intelligence. Sensitivity. Adventure.

67
00:04:25,932 --> 00:04:28,810
It's where all of us wish we'd been born.

68
00:04:28,893 --> 00:04:30,853
[lively keyboard melody playing]

69
00:04:30,937 --> 00:04:31,813
PRODUCER

70
00:04:31,896 --> 00:04:33,523
{\an8}[Fito] Luckily, they were there,

71
00:04:33,606 --> 00:04:36,901
{\an8}because they gave us the tools
to say, "Well, this is how it is."

72
00:04:38,319 --> 00:04:42,907
Please, cameraman,
focus on that fantastic graffiti.

73
00:04:42,991 --> 00:04:44,117
[men laughing]

74
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:47,745
-[Fito] It's the story of our lives.
-[man] "Failure." [laughs]

75
00:04:47,829 --> 00:04:48,705
Bastards.

76
00:04:48,788 --> 00:04:52,709
Who put that there knowing
we'd be driving by?

77
00:04:53,793 --> 00:04:55,211
[Fito] Well…

78
00:04:55,295 --> 00:04:57,547
-[Coki] You create new music every day?
-Yeah.

79
00:04:57,630 --> 00:05:00,800
-You're always inspired.
-[Fito] Wherever the night finds me.

80
00:05:00,883 --> 00:05:06,055
The country also opens
the door to other possibilities.

81
00:05:06,139 --> 00:05:09,142
It's not the same writing music
in Villa Constitución

82
00:05:09,225 --> 00:05:10,977
as it is writing here in…

83
00:05:11,060 --> 00:05:13,479
-[Fito] Where?
-What's this called?

84
00:05:13,563 --> 00:05:15,565
-London? London.
-[Fito] London Town.

85
00:05:15,648 --> 00:05:16,691
London Town.

86
00:05:16,774 --> 00:05:18,776
[playing gentle melody]

87
00:05:21,195 --> 00:05:25,074
[Fito] These days, just anyone says,
"Hey, I'll throw something together."

88
00:05:26,659 --> 00:05:30,997
Bearings, engines… Build an oven.

89
00:05:31,080 --> 00:05:32,915
Oh! [laughs]

90
00:05:34,959 --> 00:05:39,005
[Fito] Music, writing, is a craft.

91
00:05:39,088 --> 00:05:45,386
Its purpose is expressing
a feeling or feelings.

92
00:05:45,470 --> 00:05:48,056
[gentle chamber music playing]

93
00:05:51,851 --> 00:05:56,481
[Fito] And also emotional issues
from despair,

94
00:05:57,899 --> 00:06:00,193
from the desire to reveal yourself.

95
00:06:01,110 --> 00:06:03,279
And of course, from love.

96
00:06:05,114 --> 00:06:06,532
That's all.

97
00:06:06,616 --> 00:06:12,038
Let's try to have a happy ending,
even if it's just for a little while.

98
00:06:17,418 --> 00:06:21,422
<i>♪ Today the moon will embrace you ♪</i>

99
00:06:21,506 --> 00:06:22,757
Keep going.

100
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:25,510
<i>♪ Among its rays ♪</i>

101
00:06:25,593 --> 00:06:26,427
And now.

102
00:06:26,511 --> 00:06:27,678
<i>♪ Just like… ♪</i>

103
00:06:27,762 --> 00:06:29,347
Okay. Go on.

104
00:06:29,430 --> 00:06:33,017
-["Miss Understood" playing]
<i>-♪ Just ♪</i>

105
00:06:33,101 --> 00:06:39,857
-[flute playing flourish]
<i>-♪ Like Mom ♪</i>

106
00:06:43,528 --> 00:06:45,613
[music concludes]

107
00:06:47,740 --> 00:06:51,327
[in English] I think we got it. [laughs]

108
00:06:51,411 --> 00:06:53,913
-[in Spanish] Great.
-[in English] Hey, my friend.

109
00:06:53,996 --> 00:06:56,958
[director] All right.
We're gonna listen. Then we come back.

110
00:06:57,041 --> 00:06:59,752
[Fito, in Spanish] Nothing interests me
more than love.

111
00:06:59,836 --> 00:07:02,296
There's no subject matter
that interests me more.

112
00:07:02,380 --> 00:07:07,510
-[Eugenia] Thank you.
-[crowd cheers] Bravo!

113
00:07:07,593 --> 00:07:09,011
Stop it, Eugenia!

114
00:07:09,095 --> 00:07:11,472
[group laughing]

115
00:07:12,682 --> 00:07:14,016
[Eugenia] Saturday night…

116
00:07:14,100 --> 00:07:15,435
{\an8}EUGENIA KOLODZIEJ'S VOICE

117
00:07:15,518 --> 00:07:19,939
…was some sort of farewell night
because he was going on a trip.

118
00:07:20,022 --> 00:07:21,732
{\an8}"Ten years of love…

119
00:07:22,567 --> 00:07:24,402
{\an8}TEN-YEAR ANNIVERSARY
FITO AND EUGENIA

120
00:07:24,485 --> 00:07:29,073
{\an8}…fury, romance, deceit, disagreements,

121
00:07:29,157 --> 00:07:35,288
{\an8}pandemics, laughter, disappointments,
wine, pizzas, and pain."

122
00:07:36,289 --> 00:07:41,002
[Eugenia] The tour to Mexico
and Colombia was about to start.

123
00:07:41,085 --> 00:07:45,923
So, it was a bit of
a romantic farewell dinner,

124
00:07:46,007 --> 00:07:50,011
because we wouldn't see each other
for a couple of months.

125
00:07:50,094 --> 00:07:55,349
"Thank you for putting me
in my place every time I cross the line."

126
00:07:55,433 --> 00:07:56,601
[group laughing]

127
00:07:56,684 --> 00:07:59,937
"For your smile every morning,
for your love of rock,

128
00:08:01,189 --> 00:08:03,900
which hardly anyone naturally has."

129
00:08:04,734 --> 00:08:08,613
[Eugenia] We came back home
and we listened to music for a while.

130
00:08:09,113 --> 00:08:11,157
-"I won't get better."
-[people laughing]

131
00:08:11,240 --> 00:08:12,200
That's a lie.

132
00:08:12,283 --> 00:08:17,580
"The only thing I could do all these years
was become more radical about my nature,

133
00:08:17,663 --> 00:08:20,750
that stubbornness that came to stay."

134
00:08:21,542 --> 00:08:23,336
-"That's enough."
-[group chuckles]

135
00:08:23,419 --> 00:08:29,300
"I hug you so tight near the cliff
that I want us both to fall there

136
00:08:29,383 --> 00:08:33,304
and keep arguing
as we fall, even after that."

137
00:08:33,387 --> 00:08:34,347
"I love you."

138
00:08:34,430 --> 00:08:35,765
[Eugenia] My love.

139
00:08:35,848 --> 00:08:37,808
[group clapping and whistling]

140
00:08:40,019 --> 00:08:44,690
[Eugenia] And around 4:30 a.m.,
I went to sleep.

141
00:08:44,774 --> 00:08:48,528
He stayed up a little longer,
listening to music.

142
00:08:51,280 --> 00:08:53,282
["Enciende el Amor" echoing]

143
00:09:14,262 --> 00:09:16,264
[eerie distortion rises]

144
00:09:20,309 --> 00:09:22,311
[wind whistling]

145
00:09:33,573 --> 00:09:36,158
-[distortion fades]
-[siren wailing in distance]

146
00:09:38,286 --> 00:09:41,706
[Eugenia] I woke up at noon.

147
00:09:42,415 --> 00:09:45,042
And I heard… [groaning]

148
00:09:46,127 --> 00:09:47,712
[groaning intensifies]

149
00:09:49,880 --> 00:09:50,881
I got up,

150
00:09:52,675 --> 00:09:56,095
and I headed to the bedroom.

151
00:09:56,178 --> 00:09:58,180
I realized it wasn't coming from there.

152
00:09:58,264 --> 00:10:03,227
{\an8}When I got to the stairs,
I saw him lying on the landing.

153
00:10:04,562 --> 00:10:06,397
And my heart sank.

154
00:10:11,068 --> 00:10:15,281
So I started asking,
"Honey, are you okay?"

155
00:10:15,364 --> 00:10:20,161
And he was awake,
but he didn't respond to my questions.

156
00:10:20,244 --> 00:10:23,414
The first thing I did
was call an ambulance.

157
00:10:23,497 --> 00:10:25,791
"I don't get what's going on."
"What's wrong?"

158
00:10:25,875 --> 00:10:29,086
"I don't know. I found him lying there.
I don't know if he fell."

159
00:10:29,170 --> 00:10:31,088
"I don't know if it's a heart attack."

160
00:10:31,172 --> 00:10:35,176
It was such an uncertain
and desperate situation

161
00:10:35,259 --> 00:10:37,720
that I was losing my mind.

162
00:10:38,304 --> 00:10:40,097
[Fito groaning]

163
00:10:53,194 --> 00:10:57,615
[Eugenia] He screamed
until 5:00 or 5:30 a.m.

164
00:10:57,698 --> 00:11:00,326
Then he stopped screaming

165
00:11:00,409 --> 00:11:04,789
because I saw him
with his head hanging, pale,

166
00:11:04,872 --> 00:11:07,792
with dry and cracked lips.

167
00:11:07,875 --> 00:11:10,795
And I thought, "He's giving up."

168
00:11:11,879 --> 00:11:15,841
[Fito] The doctors said
they thought I'd been hit by a car.

169
00:11:15,925 --> 00:11:18,344
They never thought
I'd fallen down the stairs.

170
00:11:19,136 --> 00:11:21,389
I had 11 broken ribs.

171
00:11:21,472 --> 00:11:23,516
It was a pretty brutal situation.

172
00:11:24,975 --> 00:11:26,644
The pain was such…

173
00:11:27,895 --> 00:11:30,481
It was so severe, so intense,

174
00:11:31,023 --> 00:11:32,942
that I thought, "Well, this is it."

175
00:11:34,443 --> 00:11:36,821
"Life brought me here,
or I brought myself here,

176
00:11:36,904 --> 00:11:39,240
or circumstances brought me here."

177
00:11:39,740 --> 00:11:42,118
"And this is what it's all about."

178
00:11:42,660 --> 00:11:47,039
"Dying in a God-forsaken hospital
in a different country."

179
00:11:48,582 --> 00:11:50,751
But I had never given up before.

180
00:11:56,424 --> 00:11:59,802
[somber music playing]

181
00:12:04,306 --> 00:12:08,185
[Fito] One of the first smells I knew
was the smell of death.

182
00:12:09,395 --> 00:12:13,733
On Saturdays, my dad used
to take me to the cemetery at noon,

183
00:12:13,816 --> 00:12:15,568
before lunch,

184
00:12:15,651 --> 00:12:17,403
to see my mom's tombstone.

185
00:12:18,112 --> 00:12:22,408
And something disturbing
would happen there, you know?

186
00:12:22,491 --> 00:12:24,910
He'd kiss the photo with his hand

187
00:12:24,994 --> 00:12:27,371
and then he'd ask me to do the same.

188
00:12:28,372 --> 00:12:29,540
Oof…

189
00:12:30,207 --> 00:12:32,418
[man] Your mom died
when you were very young.

190
00:12:32,501 --> 00:12:33,794
I was eight months old.

191
00:12:33,878 --> 00:12:36,756
I didn't know her, but I guess so.

192
00:12:36,839 --> 00:12:40,593
The body carries
those scars, right? Forever.

193
00:12:42,803 --> 00:12:45,723
My mom died because her placenta
was removed incorrectly.

194
00:12:46,265 --> 00:12:48,893
So, well, it's my fault.

195
00:12:48,976 --> 00:12:52,646
So I created a character
in <i>La puta diabla,</i>

196
00:12:52,730 --> 00:12:55,608
with which, after going overboard,
I'd tell her,

197
00:12:55,691 --> 00:12:59,153
"Mom, don't worry. I'm coming."

198
00:12:59,236 --> 00:13:00,488
"I'll die soon."

199
00:13:01,947 --> 00:13:05,242
But I started writing
a sort of image diary.

200
00:13:05,326 --> 00:13:10,372
And one thing led to another
until I finished the book after two years.

201
00:13:10,456 --> 00:13:14,251
[Matías] And when you wrote the book,
were you able to let go of some of it?

202
00:13:14,335 --> 00:13:16,378
[Fito] No. It often happens to me.

203
00:13:16,462 --> 00:13:18,923
I can explain the theory very well…

204
00:13:19,006 --> 00:13:20,341
-In the work.
-In the work.

205
00:13:20,424 --> 00:13:23,010
And then, when I go to my heart,

206
00:13:23,093 --> 00:13:25,763
something else happens, man.
It's something wilder.

207
00:13:26,972 --> 00:13:32,520
So, the self-righteousness, so to speak,
of the person sorting the image says,

208
00:13:32,603 --> 00:13:35,481
"No, I shouldn't feel this
because it's wrong."

209
00:13:35,564 --> 00:13:38,567
-[Matías] You can clarify that.
-I can do it perfectly there.

210
00:13:38,651 --> 00:13:41,195
But in life, that doesn't happen to me.

211
00:13:42,696 --> 00:13:44,114
<i>♪ Your love opened a wound ♪</i>

212
00:13:44,198 --> 00:13:47,034
<i>♪ Because everything that's good</i>
<i>For you is bad for you ♪</i>

213
00:13:47,117 --> 00:13:50,704
<i>♪ Your love changed my life</i>
<i>Like a lightning bolt forever ♪</i>

214
00:13:50,788 --> 00:13:54,124
<i>♪ For what was and what will be ♪</i>

215
00:13:55,209 --> 00:13:58,963
<i>♪ What was and what will be ♪</i>

216
00:14:01,006 --> 00:14:05,594
<i>♪ The ball on the piano</i>
<i>The morning we stopped singing ♪</i>

217
00:14:05,678 --> 00:14:07,471
<i>♪ Death came one day ♪</i>

218
00:14:07,555 --> 00:14:12,268
<i>♪ And swept everything away</i>
<i>It was such a gale ♪</i>

219
00:14:13,727 --> 00:14:16,647
<i>♪ And it was a strong gale ♪</i>

220
00:14:16,730 --> 00:14:20,401
[Eugenia] Rodolf,
sleeping in short bursts,

221
00:14:20,484 --> 00:14:22,528
but in a lot of pain,

222
00:14:22,611 --> 00:14:25,739
told me, "From now on,
I don't want to know anything."

223
00:14:25,823 --> 00:14:29,159
"I don't want the phone.
I don't want to find out anything."

224
00:14:29,243 --> 00:14:32,788
Everything was postponed until October.

225
00:14:35,249 --> 00:14:39,753
This Saturday, he was going to perform
at the Zócalo in Mexico City for free,

226
00:14:39,837 --> 00:14:44,383
but he had to cancel this
and other performances in the capital…

227
00:14:44,466 --> 00:14:49,096
[reporter] <i>…due to an accident</i>
<i>where he broke five ribs.</i>

228
00:14:49,179 --> 00:14:53,392
{\an8}[reporter] <i>Remember that</i>
<i>Fito Páez is working on his latest album,</i>

229
00:14:53,475 --> 00:14:56,437
{\an8}<i>which will be called </i>Novela,
<i>and he's been working on it…</i>

230
00:14:56,520 --> 00:15:01,233
<i>♪ In the same place</i>
<i>And under the same skin ♪</i>

231
00:15:01,317 --> 00:15:05,279
<i>♪ And in the same ceremony ♪</i>

232
00:15:05,362 --> 00:15:06,864
<i>♪ I ask you for a favor ♪</i>

233
00:15:06,947 --> 00:15:09,742
<i>♪ Don't let me fall ♪</i>

234
00:15:09,825 --> 00:15:16,790
<i>♪ Into the graves of glory ♪</i>

235
00:15:16,874 --> 00:15:21,086
<i>♪ Glory ♪</i>

236
00:15:21,170 --> 00:15:25,257
<i>♪ Glory ♪</i>

237
00:15:25,341 --> 00:15:32,348
<i>♪ Glory ♪</i>

238
00:15:36,060 --> 00:15:38,354
[heart monitor beeping]

239
00:15:38,437 --> 00:15:41,398
["La música de los sueños
de tu juventud" playing]

240
00:15:41,482 --> 00:15:45,527
[Fito] After my mom died,
my dad… this is what I was told,

241
00:15:45,611 --> 00:15:47,947
spent lots of time holed up in his room.

242
00:15:48,030 --> 00:15:49,323
<i>♪ The sun rises ♪</i>

243
00:15:49,406 --> 00:15:56,038
<i>♪ And the teddy bear or your beloved</i>
<i>Pinocchio are no longer by your side ♪</i>

244
00:15:56,747 --> 00:16:00,334
My Grandma Belia and Aunt Pepa lived
with us at our house.

245
00:16:01,085 --> 00:16:03,712
They were my mothers in the real world.

246
00:16:03,796 --> 00:16:05,881
I owe them everything I am.

247
00:16:06,924 --> 00:16:10,135
["La música de los sueños
de tu juventud" continues]

248
00:16:11,637 --> 00:16:14,223
We lived in a neighborhood
in downtown Rosario,

249
00:16:14,306 --> 00:16:15,891
at 681 Balcarce Street.

250
00:16:17,101 --> 00:16:18,769
Beautiful things happened.

251
00:16:19,728 --> 00:16:21,855
My dad would take the TV out to the curb

252
00:16:21,939 --> 00:16:24,733
and sit on a chair,
with his hair neatly combed.

253
00:16:24,817 --> 00:16:27,403
He'd put on an elegant robe, you know?

254
00:16:27,903 --> 00:16:29,947
And he'd say, "Fito, bring the basin."

255
00:16:30,572 --> 00:16:34,284
And he'd put his feet in there.
He felt happy.

256
00:16:35,577 --> 00:16:39,206
Meanwhile, I'd play soccer
with my friends while he watched me.

257
00:16:40,040 --> 00:16:41,542
And the day turned into night.

258
00:16:41,625 --> 00:16:45,212
<i>♪ You fade away in the wind ♪</i>

259
00:16:45,295 --> 00:16:52,302
<i>♪ We float away in time ♪</i>

260
00:17:00,728 --> 00:17:02,896
-[Carlos] Good morning.
-[woman 1] Room 26?

261
00:17:02,980 --> 00:17:07,526
-[Carlos] No. I can go there.
-[woman 2] Oh, no. Sorry.

262
00:17:07,609 --> 00:17:10,821
-[Carlos] I can go to room 26.
-[woman 1] No.

263
00:17:11,947 --> 00:17:16,243
I'll tell you something.
Mine can be fixed. Cured. Yours can't.

264
00:17:16,326 --> 00:17:18,287
[Carlos laughs]

265
00:17:20,080 --> 00:17:23,542
What a beautiful thing, please.

266
00:17:24,460 --> 00:17:28,964
Besides, the sound
of the epode is amazing.

267
00:17:29,048 --> 00:17:30,758
Well, yeah.

268
00:17:30,841 --> 00:17:32,134
It's amazing.

269
00:17:32,217 --> 00:17:35,387
["Raros peinados nuevos"
by Charly García playing]

270
00:17:37,973 --> 00:17:41,393
This is Studio B.
We'll record the strings here.

271
00:17:41,477 --> 00:17:44,521
-But this doesn't look like a studio.
-Yeah.

272
00:17:44,605 --> 00:17:47,816
-Didn't you want a spacious studio?
-These are studio doors.

273
00:17:47,900 --> 00:17:49,777
-You can master here.
-Oh, look.

274
00:17:50,277 --> 00:17:52,780
-So you have everything close.
-I like it, Carlos.

275
00:17:52,863 --> 00:17:53,781
Good.

276
00:17:53,864 --> 00:17:57,117
I didn't know there was a studio
in Madrid in such bad shape.

277
00:17:57,201 --> 00:17:58,702
-Did you get tired?
-It hurts.

278
00:17:58,786 --> 00:18:01,914
-Of course.
-No, I didn't get tired. It hurts.

279
00:18:07,544 --> 00:18:12,633
I want to see if moving the bag
wakes him up, like a cat.

280
00:18:18,889 --> 00:18:21,225
How nice.

281
00:18:21,308 --> 00:18:23,352
Hey, honey.

282
00:18:28,023 --> 00:18:31,944
[Fito] The real test always happens

283
00:18:32,027 --> 00:18:37,241
in the recovery phase,
at the very tail end.

284
00:18:38,909 --> 00:18:43,288
Will I be able to sit
at the piano and move my arms freely?

285
00:18:43,789 --> 00:18:45,124
Will I be able to do it?

286
00:18:45,624 --> 00:18:47,835
Will I be able to move my finger again?

287
00:18:48,460 --> 00:18:49,628
What if I can't?

288
00:18:50,671 --> 00:18:52,673
[airplane engine roars]

289
00:19:02,224 --> 00:19:06,145
[Fito] "Accidents will happen,"
my friend Costello said.

290
00:19:06,228 --> 00:19:10,607
Physical pain, physical impossibility,
gives you a dose of humility.

291
00:19:10,691 --> 00:19:15,612
It reminds you that you're
a tiny little thing in the universe.

292
00:19:15,696 --> 00:19:16,738
[shower running]

293
00:19:16,822 --> 00:19:21,034
A turn in the forever young
that one always maintains, right?

294
00:19:21,118 --> 00:19:25,038
-[audience cheering]
-[Fito shouting inaudibly]

295
00:19:25,122 --> 00:19:26,874
Well, no.

296
00:19:26,957 --> 00:19:28,834
Not forever young, my friends.

297
00:19:43,223 --> 00:19:45,392
[utensils clinking]

298
00:19:48,270 --> 00:19:50,689
{\an8}[Roma] What happens
to us is much less important

299
00:19:50,772 --> 00:19:52,357
{\an8}than we like to believe.

300
00:19:53,317 --> 00:19:54,693
{\an8}There isn't just one life.

301
00:19:55,777 --> 00:19:57,279
There's only one life.

302
00:19:57,362 --> 00:20:00,282
But within that life, you live many lives,

303
00:20:00,365 --> 00:20:01,658
each a different one.

304
00:20:02,826 --> 00:20:05,537
Some are better. Some are worse.

305
00:20:10,083 --> 00:20:12,002
None of them make much sense.

306
00:20:12,085 --> 00:20:13,712
[somber music playing]

307
00:20:26,225 --> 00:20:28,227
[playing a soft melody]

308
00:20:36,443 --> 00:20:39,821
[playing "Del 63"]

309
00:21:50,475 --> 00:21:51,893
My finger works.

310
00:21:52,602 --> 00:21:53,979
My finger works.

311
00:21:55,188 --> 00:21:59,192
My finger works. It was numb.
I was worried that my hand wouldn't work.

312
00:21:59,276 --> 00:22:01,695
Because this finger is kind of…

313
00:22:02,362 --> 00:22:07,492
Because they put so many IVs in my veins
while I was at the hospital,

314
00:22:07,576 --> 00:22:10,245
that they end up numbing…

315
00:22:10,329 --> 00:22:16,209
Well, in this case, my finger.
And it almost reaches my wrist.

316
00:22:16,293 --> 00:22:17,753
But I can still play.

317
00:22:21,340 --> 00:22:25,218
I'm not saying I can play like Monk, but…

318
00:22:25,302 --> 00:22:27,804
[plays a cheerful melody]

319
00:22:27,888 --> 00:22:29,765
[clears throat] I don't know.

320
00:22:29,848 --> 00:22:33,226
It's the first time I've sung in months.

321
00:22:34,394 --> 00:22:35,604
Imagine that.

322
00:22:36,730 --> 00:22:39,900
There's still a long way to go.
This needs to open up more.

323
00:22:42,402 --> 00:22:47,532
So that the authentic
Rosario Indian can emerge.

324
00:22:50,035 --> 00:22:52,037
[slurps]

325
00:22:58,960 --> 00:23:01,338
That's what I was told I shouldn't do.

326
00:23:01,421 --> 00:23:02,964
-[man] Make that noise?
-Yeah.

327
00:23:03,048 --> 00:23:04,674
-At home?
-Yeah.

328
00:23:04,758 --> 00:23:05,801
Right.

329
00:23:06,593 --> 00:23:07,928
[slurps loudly]

330
00:23:08,011 --> 00:23:09,471
And did you make it?

331
00:23:09,554 --> 00:23:10,806
Of course!

332
00:23:12,974 --> 00:23:14,184
[clears throat]

333
00:23:14,267 --> 00:23:17,145
And who told you that? Your grandmothers?

334
00:23:17,229 --> 00:23:18,730
Or your dad?

335
00:23:18,814 --> 00:23:21,858
Yeah, my dad. Everyone.

336
00:23:21,942 --> 00:23:23,777
There was a way of…

337
00:23:24,778 --> 00:23:29,032
You had to keep up appearances,
remain elegant, follow etiquette.

338
00:23:29,116 --> 00:23:33,745
I couldn't go to a stranger's house
and eat soup like that.

339
00:23:33,829 --> 00:23:35,372
Or drink tea like that.

340
00:23:35,956 --> 00:23:37,457
It was vulgar.

341
00:23:38,041 --> 00:23:39,084
Well,

342
00:23:39,793 --> 00:23:43,630
it seems like my nature
is a bit more vulgar.

343
00:23:46,133 --> 00:23:49,344
[nostalgic music playing]

344
00:24:04,568 --> 00:24:06,445
[Fito] When you're raised with love,

345
00:24:06,528 --> 00:24:10,490
you have half
or part of your life figured out.

346
00:24:14,494 --> 00:24:18,707
If you got sick, they'd take care
of you and bundle you up.

347
00:24:19,416 --> 00:24:20,792
You had food every day.

348
00:24:20,876 --> 00:24:23,003
"Come on, Rodolfito."

349
00:24:23,086 --> 00:24:24,421
The school smock.

350
00:24:24,504 --> 00:24:28,341
The clothes, the bath.

351
00:24:29,217 --> 00:24:31,386
My dad, making me listen to music.

352
00:24:31,470 --> 00:24:34,097
I mean, all of that is love.

353
00:24:38,143 --> 00:24:39,311
In my case,

354
00:24:40,228 --> 00:24:41,396
it was a love overload.

355
00:24:45,609 --> 00:24:49,362
My dad was the general director
of the municipality of Rosario.

356
00:24:50,780 --> 00:24:53,617
He worked nonstop all day.

357
00:24:56,745 --> 00:25:00,749
I guess he was trying
to escape everything he was feeling

358
00:25:00,832 --> 00:25:02,501
after my mom's death.

359
00:25:04,961 --> 00:25:07,339
[nostalgic music continues]

360
00:25:07,964 --> 00:25:11,176
He was a very mystical, very crazy guy.

361
00:25:12,177 --> 00:25:15,430
What I mean is he worked
in a Kafkaesque office, like <i>The Trial</i>,

362
00:25:15,514 --> 00:25:17,474
and he'd come home and play Gershwin.

363
00:25:18,517 --> 00:25:19,559
Um…

364
00:25:19,643 --> 00:25:25,857
He went from the extraordinary
tango orchestras of Horacio Salgán

365
00:25:25,941 --> 00:25:27,984
to Simón Díaz in Venezuela,

366
00:25:28,068 --> 00:25:31,446
to the Fattoruso in Uruguay.

367
00:25:31,530 --> 00:25:36,117
The effect that music has on your memory.

368
00:25:36,201 --> 00:25:40,539
To this day, I hear the sound
of Herb Alpert's trumpet

369
00:25:40,622 --> 00:25:42,874
and I go straight to the scene with my dad

370
00:25:42,958 --> 00:25:47,045
in the living room in Rosario.

371
00:25:50,465 --> 00:25:54,052
At the same time,
he's exposing me to literature

372
00:25:54,135 --> 00:25:55,845
and taking me to the movies.

373
00:25:58,890 --> 00:26:03,311
He took me to see <i>2001 </i>in 1970
when I was 7 years old.

374
00:26:04,813 --> 00:26:07,232
{\an8}He made me watch <i>The Poseidon Adventure.</i>

375
00:26:07,899 --> 00:26:11,194
{\an8}[in English] I started
to believe in your promises!

376
00:26:11,278 --> 00:26:13,363
That we had a chance!

377
00:26:13,863 --> 00:26:15,323
What chance?

378
00:26:15,991 --> 00:26:21,204
You took from me the only thing
I ever loved in the whole world.

379
00:26:22,372 --> 00:26:23,665
My Linda!

380
00:26:25,542 --> 00:26:28,003
[Fito, in Spanish] I'd say that in a way,

381
00:26:28,628 --> 00:26:32,674
he set me on the path to freedom,

382
00:26:32,757 --> 00:26:34,301
in a sense.

383
00:26:35,969 --> 00:26:37,345
No, not that one.

384
00:26:38,722 --> 00:26:40,265
[random piano notes playing]

385
00:26:43,893 --> 00:26:45,895
[band rehearsing]

386
00:26:47,772 --> 00:26:50,483
{\an8}4030 REHEARSAL

387
00:26:50,567 --> 00:26:52,736
{\an8}Good afternoon, everyone.

388
00:26:53,987 --> 00:26:57,741
[playing "Las tardes del sol,
las noches del agua"]

389
00:27:04,789 --> 00:27:05,832
Euge!

390
00:27:05,915 --> 00:27:07,292
Hey!

391
00:27:11,004 --> 00:27:12,589
You're demanding…

392
00:27:14,132 --> 00:27:15,508
Hi!

393
00:27:16,801 --> 00:27:18,219
[Fito] Um…

394
00:27:19,220 --> 00:27:21,598
Since I want to take care of my throat,

395
00:27:21,681 --> 00:27:24,267
there won't be any instructions today.

396
00:27:24,351 --> 00:27:26,936
Today we'll do the first real run-through.

397
00:27:28,313 --> 00:27:32,567
Then we'll take out
our notebooks and pencils.

398
00:27:32,651 --> 00:27:35,236
[energetic rock music playing]

399
00:27:48,166 --> 00:27:51,294
[inaudible]

400
00:28:08,937 --> 00:28:10,480
["She's Mine" playing]

401
00:28:10,563 --> 00:28:14,818
<i>♪ Today I arrived in Madrid ♪</i>

402
00:28:14,901 --> 00:28:18,738
<i>♪ And by chance ♪</i>

403
00:28:19,656 --> 00:28:24,202
<i>♪ By chance ♪</i>

404
00:28:24,285 --> 00:28:26,496
<i>♪ I think of you ♪</i>

405
00:28:30,041 --> 00:28:31,292
[music stops]

406
00:28:32,210 --> 00:28:33,378
You screwed up.

407
00:28:33,461 --> 00:28:36,381
Turn up the keyboard
and turn down the guitar.

408
00:28:37,424 --> 00:28:40,343
It's one single instruction.

409
00:28:40,969 --> 00:28:42,637
Just one.

410
00:28:42,721 --> 00:28:45,014
Play loud.

411
00:28:45,849 --> 00:28:47,267
Loud.

412
00:28:47,767 --> 00:28:51,187
With the volume you have right now.

413
00:28:51,271 --> 00:28:54,399
-[man] I thought…
-It's a big difference in concept.

414
00:28:54,482 --> 00:28:57,569
When I say "turn up the volume,"
you do a mechanical movement.

415
00:28:57,652 --> 00:29:00,864
When I ask you to play loud,
it's a physical effort.

416
00:29:00,947 --> 00:29:02,574
Intellectual and physical.

417
00:29:02,657 --> 00:29:03,825
Everything else…

418
00:29:03,908 --> 00:29:06,703
The other instruments
are in the right place

419
00:29:06,786 --> 00:29:09,706
when you are in that place.

420
00:29:12,542 --> 00:29:14,335
The beginning is… [hums]

421
00:29:14,419 --> 00:29:18,256
Why do I see you moving…?
Why are you staying on a single chord?

422
00:29:19,466 --> 00:29:21,176
Oh, because in that position…

423
00:29:22,093 --> 00:29:23,219
I'll do it there.

424
00:29:23,303 --> 00:29:24,429
Yes, of course.

425
00:29:24,512 --> 00:29:26,014
-Okay.
-Let's see what you do.

426
00:29:26,097 --> 00:29:28,099
[plays "She's Mine," changing the chord]

427
00:29:36,733 --> 00:29:39,527
Can you see
you're changing the score, Carlos?

428
00:29:39,611 --> 00:29:40,737
You can't do that.

429
00:29:40,820 --> 00:29:43,156
-No.
-Because it's easier for you.

430
00:29:43,239 --> 00:29:46,618
The part is… [hums melody]

431
00:29:46,701 --> 00:29:48,119
You can't do that.

432
00:29:48,203 --> 00:29:49,829
It's wrong. Okay?

433
00:29:49,913 --> 00:29:51,206
That's the correct sheet.

434
00:29:51,289 --> 00:29:52,624
-[Carlos] Okay.
-Okay.

435
00:29:58,379 --> 00:29:59,964
Please, focus…

436
00:30:00,048 --> 00:30:02,884
I know it's hard to focus like this,

437
00:30:02,967 --> 00:30:08,389
but since asking nicely doesn't work,
I have no choice, man.

438
00:30:08,473 --> 00:30:12,310
This is the final run-through.
Don't drive me nuts.

439
00:30:12,393 --> 00:30:15,188
I still have to sing half a concert, man.

440
00:30:16,272 --> 00:30:17,273
Come on.

441
00:30:22,403 --> 00:30:23,613
Ugh!

442
00:30:36,292 --> 00:30:42,131
I can't forget that, for me,
music is math and emotion.

443
00:30:42,215 --> 00:30:44,300
[nostalgic saxophone music playing]

444
00:30:47,846 --> 00:30:49,013
[soft piano music]

445
00:30:49,097 --> 00:30:50,723
In March 1976,

446
00:30:50,807 --> 00:30:55,019
I started secondary school
at Dante Alighieri, near my house.

447
00:30:56,521 --> 00:30:58,815
My hormones were raging, you know?

448
00:30:58,898 --> 00:31:00,441
It was an adventure.

449
00:31:00,525 --> 00:31:03,236
And on March 24,
the military coup took place.

450
00:31:03,319 --> 00:31:05,989
[Mentesana] <i>We're letting</i>
<i>the population know</i>

451
00:31:06,072 --> 00:31:07,532
<i>that military operations…</i>

452
00:31:07,615 --> 00:31:08,950
WRITING SLOGANS IS A CRIME

453
00:31:09,033 --> 00:31:12,245
[Fito] I listened to music constantly.

454
00:31:12,328 --> 00:31:14,414
And I started to study and play.

455
00:31:16,082 --> 00:31:20,461
I remember the first time
I played the <i>bombo legüero.</i>

456
00:31:20,545 --> 00:31:23,006
I realized that a lot of girls

457
00:31:24,090 --> 00:31:27,635
who had never noticed me were paying
close attention and smiling.

458
00:31:27,719 --> 00:31:30,513
I thought, "Wow. This could be something."

459
00:31:32,390 --> 00:31:34,058
An opportunity.

460
00:31:35,560 --> 00:31:38,479
Later on, García developed
and centralized it

461
00:31:38,563 --> 00:31:40,773
in the extraordinary phrase,

462
00:31:40,857 --> 00:31:42,442
"How to get girls."

463
00:31:45,320 --> 00:31:51,242
On the one hand.
And on the other hand, music was that,

464
00:31:51,326 --> 00:31:53,786
like some sort of giddiness

465
00:31:53,870 --> 00:31:58,458
or connection to a world

466
00:31:59,709 --> 00:32:02,045
free of roughness.

467
00:32:03,296 --> 00:32:05,298
[nostalgic music]

468
00:32:06,341 --> 00:32:08,092
[singing inaudibly]

469
00:32:09,636 --> 00:32:11,888
It was all sensitivity.

470
00:32:11,971 --> 00:32:14,849
And, "Wow, how beautiful this is."

471
00:32:23,816 --> 00:32:27,528
On August 7, 1976,
I remember it like it was yesterday,

472
00:32:27,612 --> 00:32:29,072
I attended my first concert.

473
00:32:29,697 --> 00:32:32,533
Charly was playing
at the Fundación Astengo Auditorium.

474
00:32:32,617 --> 00:32:35,703
-La Máquina de Hacer Pájaros?
-["Rock And Roll" playing]

475
00:32:41,125 --> 00:32:43,086
["El anillo del capitán Beto" playing]

476
00:32:46,005 --> 00:32:49,384
[Fito] The next week, Invisible
played three blocks from my house,

477
00:32:49,467 --> 00:32:51,052
Luis Alberto's band.

478
00:32:56,099 --> 00:32:58,643
[Fito] Luis and Charly set me free.

479
00:32:59,143 --> 00:33:03,272
They freed my will
and desire to make music.

480
00:33:03,356 --> 00:33:06,818
They showed me
that that's what I had to do.

481
00:33:06,901 --> 00:33:10,405
Because I found happiness,
pleasure, and joy in that.

482
00:33:10,488 --> 00:33:12,699
[audience cheering]

483
00:33:15,326 --> 00:33:16,953
Thank you very much.

484
00:33:20,039 --> 00:33:21,749
So many people, man.

485
00:33:30,800 --> 00:33:33,011
I started forming my first bands.

486
00:33:33,094 --> 00:33:36,347
It was a time of intense activity
in rehearsal rooms.

487
00:33:36,431 --> 00:33:38,516
We experimented a lot.

488
00:33:38,599 --> 00:33:40,852
We always played in the same places.

489
00:33:40,935 --> 00:33:42,061
{\an8}There weren't many.

490
00:33:42,145 --> 00:33:44,355
{\an8}But the Sala Lavardén was considered

491
00:33:44,439 --> 00:33:47,150
{\an8}the meeting place
for the Rosario music scene.

492
00:33:47,233 --> 00:33:48,776
{\an8}PROGRESSIVE BAND

493
00:33:48,860 --> 00:33:53,656
In 1980, Staff won a festival
for new bands, you know?

494
00:33:53,740 --> 00:33:57,660
Rubén Goldín and Juan Carlos Baglietto
were on the jury.

495
00:33:57,744 --> 00:34:00,496
That's when Juan called
and asked me to join his band.

496
00:34:00,580 --> 00:34:04,042
And that was like touching
a piece of heaven with your hand.

497
00:34:46,042 --> 00:34:48,753
[rock music playing]

498
00:35:33,005 --> 00:35:35,007
[rock music stops]

499
00:35:46,561 --> 00:35:48,396
It's a nice day in Buenos Aires.

500
00:35:49,230 --> 00:35:50,940
[phone ringing]

501
00:35:51,023 --> 00:35:52,441
[Fabi] <i>Hi. How's it going?</i>

502
00:35:52,525 --> 00:35:53,609
[Fito] All right.

503
00:35:53,693 --> 00:35:56,654
Yesterday's run-through was good.
Demanding.

504
00:35:56,737 --> 00:36:01,909
There were a lot of instructions
in the middle.

505
00:36:01,993 --> 00:36:05,872
So, that burned me out
for the second album.

506
00:36:06,372 --> 00:36:07,498
Today,

507
00:36:09,041 --> 00:36:10,209
I won't do it.

508
00:36:10,293 --> 00:36:13,087
I'll think about myself.

509
00:36:13,171 --> 00:36:15,214
[vocalizing while gargling]

510
00:36:17,675 --> 00:36:19,552
[Fabi plays piano notes]

511
00:36:20,386 --> 00:36:21,387
Excuse me.

512
00:36:22,513 --> 00:36:25,349
[jibbers]

513
00:36:25,433 --> 00:36:26,934
[Fabi vocalizing]

514
00:36:27,018 --> 00:36:30,146
<i>-I'll tell you what happened to me later.</i>
-[vocalizing]

515
00:36:32,064 --> 00:36:36,110
I laugh because, in the '80s,
I used this for something else, Fabi.

516
00:36:36,694 --> 00:36:38,988
<i>-Yes, of course.</i>
-[vocalizing]

517
00:36:39,071 --> 00:36:41,824
<i>But the good thing is</i>
<i>that it can have different uses.</i>

518
00:36:41,908 --> 00:36:43,826
Yeah, of course.

519
00:36:43,910 --> 00:36:46,078
<i>Forty years later. </i>[laughs]

520
00:36:47,955 --> 00:36:49,498
[vocalizing]

521
00:36:49,582 --> 00:36:51,334
<i>That's why it's honored.</i>

522
00:36:51,417 --> 00:36:55,087
Now, you blow. Before, you inhaled.

523
00:36:55,171 --> 00:36:56,839
[Fabi laughs]

524
00:36:56,923 --> 00:36:58,841
[audience] <i>Olé, olé, olé, olé…</i>

525
00:36:58,925 --> 00:37:03,012
[Fito] We were presenting
<i>Actuar para vivir </i>with Baglietto.

526
00:37:03,095 --> 00:37:06,182
And there was a buzz in the hallways.

527
00:37:06,265 --> 00:37:07,850
Some sort of commotion.

528
00:37:08,726 --> 00:37:11,312
And suddenly, I see Charly.

529
00:37:11,395 --> 00:37:14,398
Formidable, well-dressed.

530
00:37:14,482 --> 00:37:16,192
And he sat next to me.

531
00:37:16,692 --> 00:37:19,654
And he asked,
"Why are you so hostile to me?"

532
00:37:20,154 --> 00:37:23,866
I froze, man. I couldn't believe it.

533
00:37:23,950 --> 00:37:25,826
I knelt at his feet.

534
00:37:25,910 --> 00:37:31,374
Right there, I said, "Sir, I love
your music more than anything."

535
00:37:32,041 --> 00:37:34,961
He hugged me,
lifted me up, and lit a joint.

536
00:37:35,044 --> 00:37:36,212
We laughed.

537
00:37:36,921 --> 00:37:38,798
A few days later, I got a call.

538
00:37:38,881 --> 00:37:41,133
Charly wanted me in his new band.

539
00:37:43,219 --> 00:37:46,389
And when I was signing the contract
in the manager's office,

540
00:37:47,139 --> 00:37:50,434
at one point, the door opened,

541
00:37:50,935 --> 00:37:54,438
and I heard an unreal voice.

542
00:37:55,106 --> 00:37:58,484
["Fue amor" playing]

543
00:37:58,567 --> 00:38:02,238
[Fito] I turned around slowly,
and there was Fabi Cantilo.

544
00:38:03,990 --> 00:38:06,117
She was an angel from another planet.

545
00:38:08,077 --> 00:38:09,996
She was intimidating.

546
00:38:10,871 --> 00:38:13,082
I fell for her right then and there.

547
00:38:13,165 --> 00:38:17,295
-["Brilliante sobre el mic" playing]
<i>-♪ There are ♪</i>

548
00:38:17,378 --> 00:38:21,882
<i>♪ Memories I won't erase ♪</i>

549
00:38:21,966 --> 00:38:26,220
<i>♪ People I won't forget ♪</i>

550
00:38:26,304 --> 00:38:30,099
<i>♪ Ooh ♪</i>

551
00:38:30,891 --> 00:38:31,851
<i>♪ There are… ♪</i>

552
00:38:31,934 --> 00:38:34,395
How many nights you made me suffer.

553
00:38:35,771 --> 00:38:39,567
<i>♪ …scents I want to take with me ♪</i>

554
00:38:39,650 --> 00:38:44,447
<i>♪ Silences I prefer to keep ♪</i>

555
00:38:44,530 --> 00:38:47,908
<i>♪ Ooh, oh-oh… ♪</i>

556
00:38:48,868 --> 00:38:54,832
[Fito] And after that, the band and I
went in two taxis to Charly's apartment.

557
00:38:56,334 --> 00:38:59,128
And right off the bat,
we listened to <i>Clics modernos.</i>

558
00:38:59,211 --> 00:39:01,964
["Ojos de video tape" by
Charly García playing]

559
00:39:04,175 --> 00:39:08,137
I could say my whole life was divided
into before and after that moment.

560
00:39:11,182 --> 00:39:14,518
[Fito] I saw
how that machine started working.

561
00:39:14,602 --> 00:39:18,856
You are playing with García
at the peak of his career.

562
00:39:18,939 --> 00:39:20,066
You know? It was…

563
00:39:20,149 --> 00:39:22,985
It was like being
in Goya's workshop, you know?

564
00:39:23,069 --> 00:39:25,696
Or in the Beatles' studio. I mean…

565
00:39:25,780 --> 00:39:27,948
I'm not exaggerating.

566
00:39:28,032 --> 00:39:32,161
[Charly] The face. Hurry up.
You're running out of time, man.

567
00:39:32,995 --> 00:39:34,955
-How did it go with…?
-Bad.

568
00:39:35,456 --> 00:39:38,042
-It was all bad.
-[Charly] Come on. We came to play.

569
00:39:39,460 --> 00:39:41,253
[speaking Italian]

570
00:39:41,337 --> 00:39:42,546
-Music.
-[Charly] Right.

571
00:39:42,630 --> 00:39:45,049
["Nada del mundo real" playing]

572
00:39:58,896 --> 00:40:02,400
-Stop there. Stop there.
-[music stops]

573
00:40:02,483 --> 00:40:06,987
[hums a melody]

574
00:40:07,071 --> 00:40:10,074
It stops there.
That's what you have to do in crescendo.

575
00:40:10,157 --> 00:40:12,159
Both together. You don't play there.

576
00:40:12,243 --> 00:40:13,744
[man] I do the…

577
00:40:13,828 --> 00:40:16,580
Okay, then join in there. Go.

578
00:40:16,664 --> 00:40:19,250
["Nada del mundo real" playing]

579
00:40:23,629 --> 00:40:26,757
I don't want to give too many instructions
on that part

580
00:40:26,841 --> 00:40:28,926
because I'll be on the other side.

581
00:40:29,009 --> 00:40:31,679
-[Carlos] Right.
-I want you to get it, basically.

582
00:40:31,762 --> 00:40:35,474
This is great.
Everything you tell us is useful.

583
00:40:37,101 --> 00:40:39,979
[Carlos] One, two, three.

584
00:42:41,642 --> 00:42:43,644
[music continues]

585
00:43:09,461 --> 00:43:12,673
The concerts start tomorrow.

586
00:43:12,756 --> 00:43:14,174
This was the last rehearsal.

587
00:43:14,258 --> 00:43:18,679
I need to rest, focus, and shut up.

588
00:43:27,229 --> 00:43:28,564
[music stops]

589
00:43:28,647 --> 00:43:30,983
You go all out with the cymbal.

590
00:43:32,568 --> 00:43:34,945
It's very close to this.

591
00:43:35,029 --> 00:43:39,742
Very close. The… [hums]

592
00:43:39,825 --> 00:43:42,786
That's his heart opening up, man.

593
00:43:44,580 --> 00:43:47,708
["Un rosarino en Budapest" playing]

594
00:43:53,922 --> 00:43:57,134
Jorge Portunato was
an important man in my life

595
00:43:57,217 --> 00:43:58,636
for many reasons.

596
00:43:58,719 --> 00:44:01,305
He was the artistic director of EMI-Odeon.

597
00:44:03,807 --> 00:44:07,436
He offered me
my first contract as a solo artist.

598
00:44:08,145 --> 00:44:11,940
What more could a 21-year-old want
than to be on the Beatles's label?

599
00:44:12,024 --> 00:44:13,317
You know?

600
00:44:18,572 --> 00:44:21,116
So, we recorded <i>Del 63</i>.

601
00:44:21,200 --> 00:44:23,619
It was mixed,
and it was quite well received.

602
00:44:23,702 --> 00:44:27,164
Looking back, I think it was
a way of introducing myself.

603
00:44:27,247 --> 00:44:28,749
Like, "Here I am."

604
00:44:44,014 --> 00:44:48,852
Another afternoon, I crossed paths
with another lightning bolt in my life.

605
00:44:49,770 --> 00:44:52,231
And I saw this indescribable being.

606
00:44:52,314 --> 00:44:54,983
He points at me in the crowd,
looks at me, and says,

607
00:44:55,067 --> 00:44:56,985
"Are you you?"

608
00:44:57,486 --> 00:45:00,280
And I immediately ask, "And are you you?"

609
00:45:00,364 --> 00:45:04,076
{\an8}And then we hugged,
like brothers from a different life.

610
00:45:04,159 --> 00:45:05,911
{\an8}DUO OF THE YEAR
PÁEZ/SPINETTA

611
00:45:05,994 --> 00:45:08,414
[sweet piano melody playing]

612
00:45:14,712 --> 00:45:18,549
In '86, when we were recording
or about to record <i>La la la</i>

613
00:45:18,632 --> 00:45:20,134
with Luis Alberto Spinetta,

614
00:45:22,052 --> 00:45:23,637
this music came up.

615
00:45:23,721 --> 00:45:26,682
My father had passed away very recently.

616
00:45:27,266 --> 00:45:32,730
And it talks about the possibility
of romance after death

617
00:45:32,813 --> 00:45:35,232
between my mother and my father,

618
00:45:35,315 --> 00:45:39,111
there in the legions of Orion or beyond.

619
00:45:40,112 --> 00:45:44,408
<i>♪ He thought about it twice and left ♪</i>

620
00:45:44,491 --> 00:45:48,954
<i>♪ His heart like a strawberry ♪</i>

621
00:45:49,037 --> 00:45:54,001
<i>♪ Always the same thing with relatives ♪</i>

622
00:45:57,296 --> 00:46:01,717
<i>♪ He left me some records in the closet ♪</i>

623
00:46:01,800 --> 00:46:05,888
<i>♪ A silver watch and a samurai ♪</i>

624
00:46:05,971 --> 00:46:11,602
<i>♪ Everything itemized in a file ♪</i>

625
00:46:12,603 --> 00:46:17,357
<i>♪ And there they go ♪</i>

626
00:46:18,609 --> 00:46:22,362
<i>♪ Part of the air ♪</i>

627
00:46:23,322 --> 00:46:26,408
<i>♪ And there they go ♪</i>

628
00:46:27,201 --> 00:46:30,704
<i>♪ On their own ♪</i>

629
00:46:30,788 --> 00:46:34,917
<i>♪ On their own ♪</i>

630
00:46:35,000 --> 00:46:41,590
<i>♪ Now free ♪</i>

631
00:46:41,673 --> 00:46:45,928
Belia and Pepa would stay by themselves
in the house on Balcarce Street

632
00:46:46,678 --> 00:46:49,681
taking comfort in memories
to continue the journey.

633
00:46:50,390 --> 00:46:54,269
Charito and Carri are my aunt and uncle.
They'd be their guardian angels.

634
00:46:54,353 --> 00:46:57,022
I'd visit them from time
to time in Rosario.

635
00:47:02,861 --> 00:47:05,739
-…tomorrow, dear.
-[Eugenia] Perfect.

636
00:47:05,823 --> 00:47:06,740
[Fito] Explain it.

637
00:47:06,824 --> 00:47:09,660
It's okay. I need to understand
in order to organize it.

638
00:47:10,327 --> 00:47:13,205
The only thing I want, Eugenia,

639
00:47:13,288 --> 00:47:18,752
is to feel the desire to drink again.

640
00:47:18,836 --> 00:47:20,045
[man laughs]

641
00:47:20,128 --> 00:47:23,674
Which I don't feel.

642
00:47:23,757 --> 00:47:27,135
Like this hot weather, right?
"Oh, this lovely heat."

643
00:47:27,219 --> 00:47:31,473
Should I bring you a non-alcoholic beer
today and see what happens?

644
00:47:31,557 --> 00:47:33,183
-[man] No!
-No.

645
00:47:33,267 --> 00:47:34,518
Maybe tomorrow.

646
00:47:34,601 --> 00:47:37,271
Tomorrow. Or Saturday would be better.

647
00:47:39,356 --> 00:47:40,941
When the first stage ends.

648
00:47:41,817 --> 00:47:43,986
Saturday. Are you here on Saturday?

649
00:47:44,069 --> 00:47:46,071
-I'm here on Saturday.
-Saturday.

650
00:47:47,364 --> 00:47:50,158
First stage, but… I told you this.

651
00:47:50,242 --> 00:47:52,744
-Non-alcoholic beer, ice-cold.
-My first show.

652
00:47:52,828 --> 00:47:55,747
I'll accept under these conditions.

653
00:47:56,456 --> 00:48:00,252
If it's not truly ice-cold,
the way I like it…

654
00:48:00,335 --> 00:48:02,212
-You know how cold I like it.
-Yes.

655
00:48:02,296 --> 00:48:04,214
I'll switch to the other one.

656
00:48:04,298 --> 00:48:05,549
There's no…

657
00:48:05,632 --> 00:48:07,175
I'll switch immediately.

658
00:48:07,676 --> 00:48:08,844
It has to have…

659
00:48:09,553 --> 00:48:12,180
It has to be just as ice-cold or colder.

660
00:48:12,264 --> 00:48:13,265
[Eugenia] Okay.

661
00:48:31,533 --> 00:48:36,204
{\an8}MOVISTAR ARENA
4030 CONCERT

662
00:48:36,288 --> 00:48:39,041
[electrifying rock intro playing]

663
00:48:44,671 --> 00:48:47,382
-Wait. How's that?
-It hurts like hell.

664
00:48:47,466 --> 00:48:48,300
-Does it?
-Yes.

665
00:48:48,383 --> 00:48:52,387
I won't see you anymore. But you're okay.
You look good. Or does it hurt?

666
00:48:52,471 --> 00:48:56,892
We men have some orders to fulfill.

667
00:48:56,975 --> 00:48:58,518
Does it hurt right now?

668
00:48:58,602 --> 00:49:01,772
I think I'll have my first drink tonight.

669
00:49:01,855 --> 00:49:04,149
-No! You…
-After two and a half months.

670
00:49:04,232 --> 00:49:05,233
No.

671
00:49:05,317 --> 00:49:09,196
In the other interview,
you told me you'd quit all drugs.

672
00:49:09,279 --> 00:49:11,406
I was told I could drink.

673
00:49:11,490 --> 00:49:14,076
Alcohol is a drug according to NA.

674
00:49:14,159 --> 00:49:15,994
Well, Fabiana. I will drink.

675
00:49:16,078 --> 00:49:18,497
I'm not Fabiana. [laughs]

676
00:49:26,004 --> 00:49:28,590
[suspenseful music playing]

677
00:49:36,890 --> 00:49:38,475
I get asked for everything.

678
00:49:39,768 --> 00:49:41,395
Never one for me.

679
00:49:46,775 --> 00:49:49,569
{\an8}[Eugenia] One day, your face
will open and fall off.

680
00:49:49,653 --> 00:49:50,570
{\an8}[Fito] Why?

681
00:49:51,363 --> 00:49:54,074
[Eugenia] Because, honey.
You can't say that.

682
00:49:54,157 --> 00:49:56,034
-You.
-[Fito] There was never a…

683
00:49:56,118 --> 00:49:59,913
One of the most demanding
and spoiled people on Earth.

684
00:49:59,997 --> 00:50:01,957
-[Fito laughs]
-[Eugenia] In the galaxy.

685
00:50:02,040 --> 00:50:03,583
I don't have a mom.

686
00:50:03,667 --> 00:50:07,295
-[Eugenia] So what?
-People without moms get spoiled.

687
00:50:11,591 --> 00:50:13,593
[nostalgic music playing]

688
00:50:20,225 --> 00:50:22,352
[reporter] Your first solo LP was <i>Del 63</i>.

689
00:50:22,436 --> 00:50:23,937
You're recording another LP

690
00:50:24,021 --> 00:50:27,024
that apparently shows that openness.

691
00:50:27,107 --> 00:50:30,193
-Rock and roll…
-It's the same with Giros.

692
00:50:30,277 --> 00:50:32,863
[playing "Giros"]

693
00:50:32,946 --> 00:50:34,573
See? <i>Giros</i>. It's…

694
00:50:34,656 --> 00:50:37,701
There's still… The tango thing is there.

695
00:50:37,784 --> 00:50:39,953
{\an8}Or the phrasing of a <i>chacarera</i>, which is…

696
00:50:40,037 --> 00:50:43,457
{\an8}<i>♪ Who said that all is lost? ♪</i>

697
00:50:44,875 --> 00:50:48,628
{\an8}<i>♪ I come to offer my heart ♪</i>

698
00:50:48,712 --> 00:50:50,714
[audience cheering]

699
00:50:51,548 --> 00:50:54,301
["Yo vengo a ofrecer mi corazón" playing]

700
00:50:54,384 --> 00:50:56,553
<i>I come to offer my heart.</i>

701
00:50:58,263 --> 00:51:02,517
On December 6, 1985,
we presented <i>Giros </i>at Luna Park.

702
00:51:02,601 --> 00:51:04,227
It was totally packed.

703
00:51:04,311 --> 00:51:08,940
And it was a night of consecration.
You know? The people from Rosario.

704
00:51:09,024 --> 00:51:13,028
<i>♪ I come to offer my heart ♪</i>

705
00:51:14,488 --> 00:51:19,076
<i>♪ So much blood the river has taken ♪</i>

706
00:51:19,951 --> 00:51:24,915
<i>♪ I come to offer my heart ♪</i>

707
00:51:24,998 --> 00:51:27,084
<i>♪ It won't be easy ♪</i>

708
00:51:27,667 --> 00:51:30,212
<i>♪ I know what happens ♪</i>

709
00:51:30,837 --> 00:51:35,217
<i>♪ It won't be as simple as I thought ♪</i>

710
00:51:36,510 --> 00:51:37,844
<i>♪ Like opening… ♪</i>

711
00:51:37,928 --> 00:51:43,600
<i>♪ Your chest and letting out your soul ♪</i>

712
00:51:44,518 --> 00:51:48,855
<i>♪ A stab of love ♪</i>

713
00:51:49,856 --> 00:51:53,401
I wrote "Yo vengo a ofrecer mi corazón"
in a couple of hours

714
00:51:54,444 --> 00:51:58,406
in the dining room
of the house Fabi and I had

715
00:51:58,490 --> 00:52:02,119
with a DX7, a piece of paper, and a pen.

716
00:52:02,744 --> 00:52:04,246
It was one of the few songs

717
00:52:04,329 --> 00:52:06,706
where lyrics and music
appeared simultaneously.

718
00:52:06,790 --> 00:52:12,003
<i>♪ When the satellites aren't enough ♪</i>

719
00:52:12,087 --> 00:52:17,008
<i>♪ I come to offer my heart ♪</i>

720
00:52:18,176 --> 00:52:20,428
[Eugenia] I can't believe it's today.

721
00:52:20,512 --> 00:52:23,557
Two months ago,
we were stuck in a hospital.

722
00:52:23,640 --> 00:52:25,809
-I was screaming in pain.
-Yeah.

723
00:52:25,892 --> 00:52:28,770
-That's also why I'm so excited.
-And you by my side.

724
00:52:29,521 --> 00:52:30,605
Always.

725
00:52:46,121 --> 00:52:48,623
[speaking indistinctly]

726
00:52:49,708 --> 00:52:51,376
-Here?
-No, I'm sure it's here.

727
00:52:51,918 --> 00:52:53,128
No, here…

728
00:52:56,923 --> 00:52:59,634
As always, I don't want to forget

729
00:53:00,218 --> 00:53:05,140
to tell you and remind you
how important you are in my life.

730
00:53:06,016 --> 00:53:12,606
And in this very special moment,
someone sent me a lesson.

731
00:53:12,689 --> 00:53:15,525
And I'm learning it. With you.

732
00:53:15,609 --> 00:53:18,945
So, let's end the year with a party.

733
00:53:19,905 --> 00:53:21,573
[all] Let's go!

734
00:53:21,656 --> 00:53:23,408
[cheering]

735
00:53:23,491 --> 00:53:25,619
-Let's go.
-[man] Let's go!

736
00:53:31,791 --> 00:53:34,794
[Fito] This experience was very special.
These concerts.

737
00:53:36,713 --> 00:53:39,966
First, because of the comeback.
To see if I could do it or not.

738
00:53:40,508 --> 00:53:42,719
You don't know until you're on the stage.

739
00:53:45,305 --> 00:53:50,477
And then, I was in the middle
of withdrawal syndrome.

740
00:53:51,811 --> 00:53:55,190
So, I was constantly breaking down.

741
00:53:55,273 --> 00:54:00,362
At the same time, I'd sing "Normal 1,"
remember my house in Rosario, and…

742
00:54:00,445 --> 00:54:02,614
I couldn't sing in rehearsals sometimes.

743
00:54:03,740 --> 00:54:05,742
[audience cheering]

744
00:54:08,078 --> 00:54:10,622
The toxic emotional resonance,
if you will,

745
00:54:10,705 --> 00:54:13,166
of the sensitivity provided by opioids,

746
00:54:13,667 --> 00:54:17,671
combined with the emotional resonance
of the lyrics and the music.

747
00:54:19,130 --> 00:54:22,175
And those words,
transcending time and one's life.

748
00:54:22,259 --> 00:54:24,970
And the lives of all the people
who listened to it too.

749
00:54:27,013 --> 00:54:30,517
It creates an overwhelming emotion.

750
00:54:32,811 --> 00:54:34,312
[audience cheering]

751
00:54:44,781 --> 00:54:47,951
Martín Rodríguez, my friend,
had warned me about that.

752
00:54:48,868 --> 00:54:52,122
He said, "Careful.
You'll meet the kid you once were."

753
00:54:54,249 --> 00:54:56,293
"Careful with what you do then."

754
00:54:57,585 --> 00:55:00,213
["Ciudad de pobres corazones" playing]

755
00:55:17,981 --> 00:55:21,359
<i>♪ In this fucking city ♪</i>

756
00:55:22,610 --> 00:55:25,697
<i>♪ Everything burns and goes away ♪</i>

757
00:55:26,364 --> 00:55:31,119
<i>♪ Poor hearts get killed ♪</i>

758
00:55:31,202 --> 00:55:36,374
<i>♪ Poor hearts get killed ♪</i>

759
00:55:38,918 --> 00:55:40,211
<i>♪ Yeah! ♪</i>

760
00:55:41,171 --> 00:55:42,630
[music fades]

761
00:55:43,506 --> 00:55:44,591
[volume increases]

762
00:55:44,674 --> 00:55:46,801
<i>♪ I don't want to go out for a smoke ♪</i>

763
00:55:46,885 --> 00:55:50,597
<i>♪ I don't want to go out</i>
<i>On the streets with you ♪</i>

764
00:55:51,765 --> 00:55:54,267
<i>♪ I don't want to start thinking ♪</i>

765
00:55:54,351 --> 00:55:58,188
<i>♪ Who placed the dope in the old drawer ♪</i>

766
00:55:58,271 --> 00:56:00,482
<i>♪ Oh! ♪</i>

767
00:56:00,565 --> 00:56:04,069
<i>♪ Good morning, Lexotanil ♪</i>

768
00:56:04,152 --> 00:56:06,196
<i>♪ Good morning, ma'am ♪</i>

769
00:56:06,279 --> 00:56:08,239
<i>♪ Good morning, Doctor ♪</i>

770
00:56:09,157 --> 00:56:10,867
<i>♪ Damn your love ♪</i>

771
00:56:10,950 --> 00:56:15,330
<i>♪ Your immense kingdom</i>
<i>And your longed-for pain ♪</i>

772
00:56:15,413 --> 00:56:17,165
<i>♪ Oh! Oh! ♪</i>

773
00:56:22,629 --> 00:56:24,547
[song fades out]

774
00:56:24,631 --> 00:56:26,508
[unsettling music playing]

775
00:56:31,888 --> 00:56:33,765
[Fito] I was in Rio.

776
00:56:34,432 --> 00:56:38,103
That was in '86.
November 7, if I'm not mistaken.

777
00:56:39,104 --> 00:56:42,565
I'd just finished playing
at the Circo Voador that evening.

778
00:56:43,108 --> 00:56:45,819
Fortunato told me.
He was the president of EMI.

779
00:56:45,902 --> 00:56:47,987
-He was shaking.
-[phone ringing]

780
00:56:54,661 --> 00:56:57,163
[phone continues ringing]

781
00:56:59,082 --> 00:57:03,294
{\an8}FITO PÁEZ'S GRANDMOTHER
AND AUNT MURDERED

782
00:57:03,378 --> 00:57:05,755
[somber music playing]

783
00:57:05,839 --> 00:57:10,802
{\an8}NO LEADS IN THE MURDER OF THE THREE WOMEN

784
00:57:24,607 --> 00:57:26,776
[Fito] When it happened,
Charly was in Rio.

785
00:57:27,277 --> 00:57:31,698
He didn't leave my side
for the first 48 hours, I'd say.

786
00:57:31,781 --> 00:57:36,744
He took me to the beach. Stayed with me.
We didn't talk. He hugged me.

787
00:57:38,496 --> 00:57:40,373
It was a very hazy period.

788
00:57:40,457 --> 00:57:44,794
I was drinking, I don't know, two
or three bottles of whiskey per hour.

789
00:57:44,878 --> 00:57:46,796
To ease the pain, right?

790
00:57:49,215 --> 00:57:51,050
I literally went crazy.

791
00:57:54,095 --> 00:57:55,763
[Charito] Fito was in bad shape.

792
00:57:59,976 --> 00:58:02,562
Fito is very attached to his family.

793
00:58:04,481 --> 00:58:07,484
-Look at what a house means.
-Yes.

794
00:58:07,567 --> 00:58:09,444
The memories of so many years.

795
00:58:09,527 --> 00:58:10,361
{\an8}UNCLE AND AUNT

796
00:58:10,445 --> 00:58:13,364
{\an8}And it was a hard blow.

797
00:58:13,865 --> 00:58:16,284
{\an8}Of all the pain he suffered,

798
00:58:16,951 --> 00:58:18,661
{\an8}that one was terrible.

799
00:58:19,162 --> 00:58:21,748
["Track Track" playing]

800
00:58:24,209 --> 00:58:26,419
[Cantilo] Fito would cry at night.

801
00:58:26,503 --> 00:58:28,296
I didn't know how to console him.

802
00:58:28,379 --> 00:58:32,050
I'd stay by his side,
but I didn't know what to say to him.

803
00:58:32,133 --> 00:58:33,426
I didn't know what to do.

804
00:58:34,636 --> 00:58:38,264
<i>♪ Time doesn't go by, at least for me… ♪</i>

805
00:58:39,015 --> 00:58:41,684
Do you like the audience?
What do you want from them?

806
00:58:41,768 --> 00:58:43,478
No, I don't like the audience.

807
00:58:43,561 --> 00:58:46,356
I don't like large crowds.

808
00:58:46,439 --> 00:58:48,191
I couldn't stand the world.

809
00:58:48,274 --> 00:58:51,236
I guess I didn't have
the courage to kill myself.

810
00:58:51,319 --> 00:58:53,279
I was in deep anguish.

811
00:58:54,656 --> 00:59:00,703
When you're on a balcony
with one foot out and one foot in…

812
00:59:00,787 --> 00:59:06,209
I was lucky to have Fabi
telling me, "Fito, come here."

813
00:59:06,793 --> 00:59:11,464
I pulled my leg back in
and went inside the apartment.

814
00:59:16,427 --> 00:59:19,639
And I started making the album right away.

815
00:59:19,722 --> 00:59:21,975
["Ciudad de pobres corazones" playing]

816
00:59:22,058 --> 00:59:27,939
Finally, what emerges
is a powerful, undeniable riff

817
00:59:28,022 --> 00:59:31,109
that imposes metropolitan violence.

818
00:59:31,192 --> 00:59:33,570
<i>♪ Everything burns and goes away ♪</i>

819
00:59:34,988 --> 00:59:39,367
I wasn't offering my heart anymore.
What for? To have it broken?

820
00:59:39,450 --> 00:59:41,578
<i>♪ Poor hearts get killed ♪</i>

821
00:59:41,661 --> 00:59:42,954
<i>♪ Poor hearts… ♪</i>

822
00:59:43,037 --> 00:59:45,373
It was a cry of hatred.

823
00:59:45,456 --> 00:59:46,541
"Motherfuckers."

824
00:59:46,624 --> 00:59:51,921
<i>♪ Poor hearts get killed ♪</i>

825
00:59:52,547 --> 00:59:53,881
<i>♪ Yeah! ♪</i>

826
00:59:55,633 --> 00:59:58,052
There's a terrible sentence there.

827
00:59:58,136 --> 01:00:02,015
I tell God, "Damn your love, your kingdom,
and your longed-for pain."

828
01:00:02,098 --> 01:00:08,104
Sometimes I shake
when I think about how I repeat that line.

829
01:00:10,565 --> 01:00:13,484
And on the other hand,
it's a man's strength.

830
01:00:15,612 --> 01:00:20,241
He can do anything, in the sense
that he can get rid of all that.

831
01:00:22,160 --> 01:00:25,121
Maybe I'll have to scream it
until the day I die.

832
01:00:26,456 --> 01:00:29,083
To be able to coexist with that idea.

833
01:00:29,167 --> 01:00:32,086
["Ciudad de pobres corazones" continues]

834
01:01:01,240 --> 01:01:04,202
[cheering continues in the distance]

835
01:01:06,329 --> 01:01:07,955
[Fito speaks indistinctly]

836
01:01:15,129 --> 01:01:16,339
Ah…

837
01:01:18,716 --> 01:01:19,884
Should I turn on the shower?

838
01:01:19,967 --> 01:01:21,803
-No.
-No? Not yet.

839
01:01:22,804 --> 01:01:25,098
-Let me relax for a bit.
-Sure, don't worry.

840
01:01:40,154 --> 01:01:42,615
[melancholic piano music playing]

841
01:01:54,919 --> 01:01:56,170
[tone beeps]

842
01:01:56,254 --> 01:01:59,006
[Fito] <i>Mati, I just woke up.</i>

843
01:01:59,090 --> 01:02:03,720
<i>I spoke with Eugenia,</i>
<i>and we're going to separate.</i>

844
01:02:06,222 --> 01:02:08,975
<i>I'm very fragile and vulnerable, Mati.</i>

845
01:02:09,058 --> 01:02:11,394
<i>And intolerant, you know? That too.</i>

846
01:02:14,731 --> 01:02:17,483
<i>I see light in the distance…</i>

847
01:02:19,652 --> 01:02:21,404
<i>but the road ahead will be hard.</i>

848
01:02:35,042 --> 01:02:36,210
I love you.

849
01:02:36,294 --> 01:02:37,170
Are you okay?

850
01:02:37,253 --> 01:02:38,171
SON

851
01:02:39,630 --> 01:02:42,175
MEXICO CITY

852
01:02:44,051 --> 01:02:45,303
{\an8}Well, 2025 is starting.

853
01:02:45,386 --> 01:02:48,181
{\an8}The good news is
we're going back to the Zócalo.

854
01:02:48,681 --> 01:02:51,768
<i>To the Auditorio Nacional. To Guadalajara.</i>
<i>We're in Mexico.</i>

855
01:02:51,851 --> 01:02:54,854
<i>I'm so happy to start the year there.</i>

856
01:02:55,855 --> 01:03:00,318
<i>The July Spanish tour was confirmed too,</i>
<i>so we're going to have a great year.</i>

857
01:03:18,085 --> 01:03:20,171
Thank goodness, right?

858
01:03:21,631 --> 01:03:24,175
I had a daughter, but,

859
01:03:24,967 --> 01:03:29,722
apparently, I've slipped away
into oblivion.

860
01:03:29,806 --> 01:03:31,641
In a black hole.

861
01:03:32,725 --> 01:03:36,312
But at least two sentences.
"Hi, Dad. How are you?"

862
01:03:37,438 --> 01:03:40,399
Anyway… I love you.

863
01:03:56,499 --> 01:03:59,877
At some point in 1986,
I rented a house in Caballito,

864
01:03:59,961 --> 01:04:01,629
in Pasaje La Mar.

865
01:04:01,712 --> 01:04:05,091
I had brought a Prince poster,
of <i>Sign O' the Times.</i>

866
01:04:05,174 --> 01:04:08,886
It was a great laboratory.
I made <i>Ciudad de pobres corazones </i>there.

867
01:04:08,970 --> 01:04:12,682
We rehearsed <i>La la la</i>.
I wrote <i>Ey!, Novela, Tercer Mundo.</i>

868
01:04:13,182 --> 01:04:17,019
At that time, I felt I had to shake off
of the macabre tone of <i>Ciudad.</i>

869
01:04:17,979 --> 01:04:21,315
I had to move forward
and not get bogged down.

870
01:04:23,025 --> 01:04:26,821
The first nine songs were going
to be the next album, <i>Ey!</i>

871
01:04:26,904 --> 01:04:29,907
And so <i>Novela </i>was shelved for years.

872
01:04:33,077 --> 01:04:35,371
Things with Fabi were going downhill.

873
01:04:35,913 --> 01:04:39,250
I was a bit numb to it
because I was out of my mind.

874
01:04:39,333 --> 01:04:41,544
{\an8}From the disco to the thing, you know?

875
01:04:41,627 --> 01:04:43,671
{\an8}[Fito] I wasn't helping.

876
01:04:43,754 --> 01:04:47,133
{\an8}I drank all day long, played the piano,
rehearsed, recorded.

877
01:04:47,216 --> 01:04:49,802
[Cantilo] He'd call
the answering machine. "Fabi."

878
01:04:49,886 --> 01:04:52,179
-[Fito] She was a hurricane.
-[Cantilo] Drunk.

879
01:04:52,263 --> 01:04:53,514
He was already a drunk.

880
01:04:53,598 --> 01:04:56,058
[Fito] And between verses and lines,

881
01:04:56,142 --> 01:04:59,270
in the middle of all that,
the end of the romance was hiding.

882
01:04:59,353 --> 01:05:00,771
[Cantilo] I never left.

883
01:05:01,439 --> 01:05:02,607
He did.

884
01:05:03,858 --> 01:05:06,777
[Fito] What comes next is
the story of a girlfriend I had

885
01:05:06,861 --> 01:05:09,572
who always drove me crazy
and threw plates at me.

886
01:05:09,655 --> 01:05:11,157
I wrote this song for her.

887
01:05:11,741 --> 01:05:15,745
<i>♪ She went down the alley ♪</i>

888
01:05:15,828 --> 01:05:18,039
<i>♪ Where he was ♪</i>

889
01:05:20,416 --> 01:05:24,253
<i>♪ Then she threw up that rum ♪</i>

890
01:05:24,337 --> 01:05:27,214
<i>♪ Staining the wall ♪</i>

891
01:05:28,633 --> 01:05:30,885
I remember that after <i>Ey! </i>came out,

892
01:05:30,968 --> 01:05:32,219
Roly Hernández,

893
01:05:32,303 --> 01:05:36,182
in the same office
where Jorge Portunato had hired me,

894
01:05:36,265 --> 01:05:37,683
fired me from the company.

895
01:05:37,767 --> 01:05:39,977
He said they wouldn't renew my contract.

896
01:05:40,061 --> 01:05:41,604
<i>♪ …his life ♪</i>

897
01:05:41,687 --> 01:05:43,606
<i>♪ But that was okay… ♪</i>

898
01:05:43,689 --> 01:05:45,900
[Fito] So yeah, it was a shitty period.

899
01:05:45,983 --> 01:05:47,401
Without a penny to my name.

900
01:05:48,861 --> 01:05:52,615
I was reading Bukowski, I remember.
He was a great companion.

901
01:05:52,698 --> 01:05:54,325
He understood me.

902
01:05:55,242 --> 01:05:59,747
My house was a graveyard
of cigarettes and beer cans.

903
01:06:00,581 --> 01:06:05,336
One afternoon, my aunt and uncle,
Charito and Carrizo, rang my doorbell.

904
01:06:05,419 --> 01:06:07,630
They were very alarmed by what they saw.

905
01:06:07,713 --> 01:06:09,674
The whole thing was a mess.

906
01:06:09,757 --> 01:06:12,843
They took me out to dinner.
I hadn't eaten in months.

907
01:06:12,927 --> 01:06:15,721
And after that visit…
family came back into my life.

908
01:06:15,805 --> 01:06:18,099
["You Got It" by Roy Orbison playing]

909
01:06:20,851 --> 01:06:23,771
I'd go out to walk
on the street with my Discman,

910
01:06:24,772 --> 01:06:27,483
listening to "You Got It" by Roy Orbison.

911
01:06:27,566 --> 01:06:30,611
It made me somewhat happy
to see the sun again.

912
01:06:31,362 --> 01:06:33,280
That's when <i>Tercer Mundo </i>took shape.

913
01:06:33,364 --> 01:06:38,744
<i>Tercer Mundo </i>is a very rebellious album.

914
01:06:39,328 --> 01:06:42,123
Ladies and gentlemen,
I live in this neighborhood.

915
01:06:42,957 --> 01:06:44,166
They sell coke here.

916
01:06:44,250 --> 01:06:46,377
Girls go wild.

917
01:06:46,460 --> 01:06:48,295
Priests are drug dealers.

918
01:06:48,379 --> 01:06:50,047
Politicians are terrible.

919
01:06:50,131 --> 01:06:52,758
A boy who used to sell flowers
now has a gun.

920
01:06:52,842 --> 01:06:53,968
That's how it is.

921
01:06:54,051 --> 01:06:57,930
<i>♪ They met by chance in a café ♪</i>

922
01:06:58,514 --> 01:07:03,185
<i>♪ With tired souls from so much walking ♪</i>

923
01:07:04,270 --> 01:07:09,900
<i>♪ She had a carnation in her hand ♪</i>

924
01:07:10,776 --> 01:07:14,989
<i>♪ He approached her</i>
<i>And asked if she was okay ♪</i>

925
01:07:15,614 --> 01:07:20,327
<i>♪ She could barely reach</i>
<i>The window on tiptoes ♪</i>

926
01:07:20,411 --> 01:07:25,332
<i>♪ And he took her</i>
<i>For a walk down Corrientes ♪</i>

927
01:07:25,833 --> 01:07:29,253
The story of "11 y 6" is about two kids
on Corrientes Street,

928
01:07:29,336 --> 01:07:31,505
an 11-year-old boy and a 6-year-old girl,

929
01:07:31,589 --> 01:07:34,216
who simply fall in love in 1985.

930
01:07:34,300 --> 01:07:37,011
-Five years later…
-[audience cheering]

931
01:07:37,094 --> 01:07:43,267
Five years later… the government changed.
Alfonsín is gone. Menem takes over.

932
01:07:43,350 --> 01:07:48,606
Violence gets much worse on the streets.
This boy is no longer 11. He's 15.

933
01:07:48,689 --> 01:07:50,274
And this happens, right?

934
01:07:50,357 --> 01:07:52,109
["El chico de la tapa" playing]

935
01:07:52,193 --> 01:07:55,821
<i>♪ The boy on the cover was selling flowers</i>
<i>On Corrientes ♪</i>

936
01:07:56,655 --> 01:08:01,535
<i>♪ Then he lost his girl</i>
<i>In a room in some hospital ♪</i>

937
01:08:02,244 --> 01:08:06,165
<i>♪ And today, kindly</i>
<i>And with a big smile on his face ♪</i>

938
01:08:07,458 --> 01:08:11,545
{\an8}<i>♪ He stops you on the street</i>
<i>If you don't give it up, he attacks you ♪</i>

939
01:08:11,629 --> 01:08:13,172
{\an8}ZÓCALO
MEXICO CITY

940
01:08:16,133 --> 01:08:21,889
[female vendor] Mugs, 50 pesos each!
50 pesos! Mugs, 50 pesos each!

941
01:08:21,972 --> 01:08:22,973
LIVING

942
01:08:23,057 --> 01:08:24,475
WITHOUT LOVE

943
01:08:24,558 --> 01:08:25,518
LIVE

944
01:08:29,355 --> 01:08:30,481
Sorry.

945
01:08:30,564 --> 01:08:33,025
[male vendor] This is delicious!

946
01:08:37,404 --> 01:08:39,907
-[woman 1] We were following you.
-[woman 2] Yeah.

947
01:08:39,990 --> 01:08:41,951
-I texted your mom.
-And?

948
01:08:44,203 --> 01:08:45,538
I sent her a message.

949
01:08:45,621 --> 01:08:47,414
-Did she answer?
-Yes.

950
01:08:47,498 --> 01:08:49,750
-And?
-I explained everything.

951
01:08:51,043 --> 01:08:53,671
And she understood.

952
01:08:53,754 --> 01:08:56,132
She replied quickly, within an hour.

953
01:08:58,008 --> 01:09:00,219
-[Martín] Is everything okay?
-Yeah. Yeah.

954
01:09:01,554 --> 01:09:03,806
[emotional music playing]

955
01:09:18,612 --> 01:09:23,576
[Fito] The first time I saw Cecilia Roth
was at a movie theater in Madrid.

956
01:09:24,785 --> 01:09:29,206
{\an8}[woman] I want to have a party.
There'll be music, alcohol, porn, drugs…

957
01:09:29,290 --> 01:09:32,126
-[man] Hard drugs.
-[woman] Only the stimulating kind.

958
01:09:34,003 --> 01:09:38,257
I met him in José Ignacio,
Uruguay, a couple of years ago,

959
01:09:38,799 --> 01:09:43,762
at a costume party we had planned.

960
01:09:43,846 --> 01:09:46,098
And he showed up.

961
01:09:48,184 --> 01:09:51,937
I remember he weighed 55 kilos
when I met him.

962
01:09:52,021 --> 01:09:55,858
The first time we slept together,
I untangled his hair.

963
01:09:55,941 --> 01:09:59,987
I felt like it was
an act of general untangling.

964
01:10:00,070 --> 01:10:03,490
And I think he was going through
a pretty tangled-up moment.

965
01:10:03,574 --> 01:10:06,535
[Fito] She absolutely dazzled me.

966
01:10:06,619 --> 01:10:09,038
All of that was a source of inspiration

967
01:10:09,121 --> 01:10:11,665
and a vital way
to reinsert myself into life.

968
01:10:11,749 --> 01:10:16,212
[Cecilia] It was like, "I wasn't looking
for love, and I saw you," for both of us.

969
01:10:17,129 --> 01:10:20,007
[Fito] That summer,
<i>El amor después del amor </i>was released.

970
01:10:22,176 --> 01:10:23,886
[audience cheering]

971
01:10:31,894 --> 01:10:34,605
[cheering and whistling]

972
01:10:41,362 --> 01:10:45,866
Fito! Fito! Fito! Fito!

973
01:11:07,638 --> 01:11:09,682
[audience whistling]

974
01:11:14,895 --> 01:11:17,940
["El amor después del amor" playing]

975
01:11:35,291 --> 01:11:40,462
<i>♪ Maybe love after love ♪</i>

976
01:11:40,546 --> 01:11:45,342
<i>♪ Looks like this ray of sun ♪</i>

977
01:11:45,426 --> 01:11:47,970
<i>♪ And now that I searched ♪</i>

978
01:11:48,053 --> 01:11:50,180
<i>♪ And now that I found ♪</i>

979
01:11:50,764 --> 01:11:55,227
<i>♪ The perfume that pain carries ♪</i>

980
01:11:55,311 --> 01:12:00,524
<i>♪ In the essence of souls ♪</i>

981
01:12:00,607 --> 01:12:05,571
<i>♪ Every religion says ♪</i>

982
01:12:05,654 --> 01:12:09,908
<i>♪ But I think it is love ♪</i>

983
01:12:09,992 --> 01:12:13,746
<i>♪ After love ♪</i>

984
01:12:13,829 --> 01:12:15,622
[woman] <i>♪ Yeah! Yeah! ♪</i>

985
01:12:15,706 --> 01:12:19,793
[vocalizing]

986
01:12:20,377 --> 01:12:23,464
<i>♪ Yeah! Yeah! Yeah! ♪</i>

987
01:12:23,547 --> 01:12:26,467
<i>♪ Ooh, ooh! ♪</i>

988
01:12:26,550 --> 01:12:30,346
[vocalizing]

989
01:12:30,429 --> 01:12:35,809
[Fito] <i>♪ Maybe love after love ♪</i>

990
01:12:35,893 --> 01:12:40,481
<i>♪ Looks like this ray of sun ♪</i>

991
01:12:40,564 --> 01:12:43,650
<i>♪ And now that I searched ♪</i>

992
01:12:43,734 --> 01:12:46,195
<i>♪ And now that I found ♪</i>

993
01:12:46,278 --> 01:12:49,990
<i>♪ The perfume that my pain carries ♪</i>

994
01:12:51,158 --> 01:12:55,496
<i>♪ In the essence of souls ♪</i>

995
01:12:56,497 --> 01:12:59,666
<i>♪ Every religion says ♪</i>

996
01:12:59,750 --> 01:13:01,377
Oh, Fito Páez!

997
01:13:02,127 --> 01:13:03,462
What kind of Fito are you?

998
01:13:03,545 --> 01:13:06,131
Adolfito, Rodolfito, or Grafito?

999
01:13:06,215 --> 01:13:08,092
-They call me Pito Fáez.
-Pito Fáez?

1000
01:13:08,175 --> 01:13:09,885
{\an8}[reporter] Is it hard to create

1001
01:13:09,968 --> 01:13:12,221
{\an8}after the success
of <i>El amor después del amor</i>?

1002
01:13:12,304 --> 01:13:15,474
The hard part's coming here,
not being able to take two steps.

1003
01:13:16,433 --> 01:13:18,227
{\an8}HAVANA
CUBA

1004
01:13:18,310 --> 01:13:20,521
{\an8}[Fito] I was always out and about.

1005
01:13:20,604 --> 01:13:22,606
Then I couldn't go out, go for a bite…

1006
01:13:22,689 --> 01:13:25,984
I couldn't do anything.
I went from the bed to the living room.

1007
01:13:26,985 --> 01:13:30,406
{\an8}It was also fun
because it was like the Rolling Stones.

1008
01:13:30,489 --> 01:13:33,617
You'd go anywhere, and it was amazing.

1009
01:13:34,910 --> 01:13:39,123
No one is ready
for such massive popularity and success.

1010
01:13:39,665 --> 01:13:41,750
You're not born ready for that.

1011
01:14:22,040 --> 01:14:27,379
[audience] <i>♪ Olé, olé, olé, olé!</i>
<i>Fito! Fito! ♪</i>

1012
01:14:27,463 --> 01:14:33,260
<i>♪ Olé, olé, olé, olé! Fito, Fito! ♪</i>

1013
01:14:47,191 --> 01:14:49,568
[Fito] What happened
in "Alegría a mi corazón"?

1014
01:14:50,819 --> 01:14:52,488
The last modulation.

1015
01:14:53,489 --> 01:14:55,866
How many times did I tell them, man?

1016
01:14:58,785 --> 01:15:01,580
-[man] Do you care?
-[Fito] I do, man.

1017
01:15:01,663 --> 01:15:03,081
I care a lot.

1018
01:15:06,001 --> 01:15:08,295
Will it change the course
of the universe? No.

1019
01:15:08,378 --> 01:15:10,214
But I work for this to go well.

1020
01:15:10,839 --> 01:15:12,591
And if it doesn't, it bothers me.

1021
01:15:21,642 --> 01:15:23,644
[phone ringing]

1022
01:15:28,357 --> 01:15:33,153
[Fito] Diego, can you explain to me
how the whole band

1023
01:15:33,237 --> 01:15:36,323
messes up the modulation at the end?

1024
01:15:36,907 --> 01:15:40,577
<i>-Oh no. It was amazing.</i>
-No, man. It ruined my night.

1025
01:15:40,661 --> 01:15:43,747
Give them hell, man! Give them hell.

1026
01:15:43,830 --> 01:15:46,083
It's what we always say about the end.

1027
01:15:46,166 --> 01:15:49,044
If you play the last chord wrong,
the concert is shit.

1028
01:15:49,127 --> 01:15:50,295
And it was.

1029
01:15:50,796 --> 01:15:52,798
<i>-Yeah, yeah…</i>
-I can't keep it in.

1030
01:15:52,881 --> 01:15:54,466
I'm sorry, but I can't.

1031
01:15:54,550 --> 01:15:58,262
<i>No, it's okay. We just talked about it.</i>
<i>I had to take it.</i>

1032
01:15:58,345 --> 01:16:00,389
Everyone!

1033
01:16:00,472 --> 01:16:02,641
No one played it, man. No one.

1034
01:16:03,267 --> 01:16:04,268
Me neither.

1035
01:16:04,351 --> 01:16:05,602
That's what I'm saying.

1036
01:16:06,144 --> 01:16:09,356
Go to a witch, get an exorcism.

1037
01:16:09,439 --> 01:16:10,482
I don't know.

1038
01:16:10,566 --> 01:16:12,025
Sorry, Rodolfo.

1039
01:16:12,109 --> 01:16:14,820
I'll take responsibility.
I'll tell the guys.

1040
01:16:14,903 --> 01:16:17,781
Yes. We can't move forward like this.

1041
01:16:17,864 --> 01:16:20,367
It's like having… You know? It's like…

1042
01:16:20,450 --> 01:16:22,536
We can't let this slide.

1043
01:16:22,619 --> 01:16:25,831
Because we were having
a wonderful concert.

1044
01:16:25,914 --> 01:16:27,416
Simply wonderful.

1045
01:16:27,499 --> 01:16:31,211
I'm being honest, and I can't keep this
to myself at the end of a tour.

1046
01:16:32,170 --> 01:16:34,298
-No, of course not.
-Okay.

1047
01:16:34,840 --> 01:16:37,092
-Hugs.
-Okay. Kisses. Bye.

1048
01:17:00,324 --> 01:17:02,951
-Are you okay?
-Yes. I'm tired.

1049
01:17:03,910 --> 01:17:05,537
[coughs] Of what?

1050
01:17:07,539 --> 01:17:10,542
[woman] Don't say that, Martín.
What do you mean?

1051
01:17:11,627 --> 01:17:13,712
"Are you tired? What are you tired of?"

1052
01:17:13,795 --> 01:17:14,796
Sure. Of what?

1053
01:17:14,880 --> 01:17:19,384
I swear I don't even have
the strength to respond to that.

1054
01:17:19,468 --> 01:17:20,469
[woman laughs]

1055
01:17:21,053 --> 01:17:22,763
A little bit of humor.

1056
01:17:23,764 --> 01:17:26,350
[nostalgic piano music]

1057
01:17:26,433 --> 01:17:28,935
[Fito] Hi, baby. Hi.

1058
01:17:32,064 --> 01:17:34,775
-[Cecilia] Oh, what a beautiful baby.
-Mom.

1059
01:17:36,026 --> 01:17:38,111
The rock music life isn't very stable.

1060
01:17:38,779 --> 01:17:41,448
Especially for the guy doing it,

1061
01:17:41,531 --> 01:17:43,158
who's making music.

1062
01:17:46,078 --> 01:17:49,081
Your schedule gets messed up.

1063
01:17:50,123 --> 01:17:51,541
It is kind of inverted

1064
01:17:51,625 --> 01:17:55,671
and sometimes it's not easy
to have a family that way.

1065
01:17:56,797 --> 01:17:59,549
-Okay. Let's park it.
-Where?

1066
01:17:59,633 --> 01:18:01,051
-There.
-Okay.

1067
01:18:01,134 --> 01:18:02,094
Let's go there.

1068
01:18:02,177 --> 01:18:03,887
[Martín] Let's park it there.

1069
01:18:03,970 --> 01:18:05,764
[continue indistinctly]

1070
01:18:30,122 --> 01:18:31,665
[Fito] Growing up is weird.

1071
01:18:32,165 --> 01:18:34,167
[melancholic music]

1072
01:18:37,254 --> 01:18:39,673
You're a different person every day.

1073
01:18:41,466 --> 01:18:42,509
What's that?

1074
01:18:42,592 --> 01:18:43,719
When you also think,

1075
01:18:43,802 --> 01:18:47,681
"Just yesterday, I was playing soccer
in Plaza San Martín with my friends."

1076
01:18:49,725 --> 01:18:53,854
"And now I'm playing for 80,000 people
in Mexico. How is that possible?"

1077
01:18:54,438 --> 01:18:55,981
Well…

1078
01:18:57,441 --> 01:18:58,900
the passage of time.

1079
01:19:23,383 --> 01:19:26,011
[serene piano music playing]

1080
01:19:28,430 --> 01:19:30,432
[Fito] I didn't know this would happen.

1081
01:19:32,017 --> 01:19:33,894
What you do know later

1082
01:19:34,936 --> 01:19:37,564
is that time has passed,

1083
01:19:38,064 --> 01:19:40,275
and you did something right.

1084
01:19:43,236 --> 01:19:45,113
You're 61 years old.

1085
01:19:45,197 --> 01:19:46,364
You raised two kids.

1086
01:19:47,824 --> 01:19:49,242
You did everything you did.

1087
01:19:49,326 --> 01:19:51,787
And that, you don't know how…

1088
01:19:53,914 --> 01:19:59,419
is something you see
when people give you that recognition.

1089
01:19:59,503 --> 01:20:01,588
And there's no one there.

1090
01:20:01,671 --> 01:20:03,089
You're on your own, man.

1091
01:20:04,090 --> 01:20:06,927
[serene, emotional music continues]

1092
01:20:35,413 --> 01:20:37,290
[in English] Suicidal tendencies.

1093
01:20:42,254 --> 01:20:45,882
[in Spanish] When those thoughts
haunt me, I let them be.

1094
01:20:47,634 --> 01:20:48,844
[man] Do they haunt you?

1095
01:20:49,719 --> 01:20:50,846
They do.

1096
01:20:51,763 --> 01:20:53,765
In times like these, they do haunt me.

1097
01:21:00,647 --> 01:21:03,817
There's something in me
that doesn't belong in this world.

1098
01:21:03,900 --> 01:21:06,027
[serene music continues]

1099
01:21:09,447 --> 01:21:11,283
All your friends are famous.

1100
01:21:11,366 --> 01:21:14,202
Others pay attention
to everything you say.

1101
01:21:17,789 --> 01:21:18,623
It's a hassle.

1102
01:21:18,707 --> 01:21:20,709
I mean, that's not your life.

1103
01:21:26,631 --> 01:21:30,927
When I couldn't do certain things,
the idea started to overwhelm me.

1104
01:21:32,220 --> 01:21:35,181
And the first traumatic event, I'd say,

1105
01:21:35,265 --> 01:21:38,143
was when I wanted
to stop after <i>El amor después del amor</i>

1106
01:21:38,226 --> 01:21:42,105
and both the label
and my manager at the time said no.

1107
01:21:43,106 --> 01:21:45,650
It was the only time
I was forced to make an album.

1108
01:21:52,449 --> 01:21:56,578
[young Fito] Look,
the plan with this album was…

1109
01:21:56,661 --> 01:21:57,579
I mean,

1110
01:21:57,662 --> 01:22:02,083
everything that happened around my work
was very explosive to the outside world.

1111
01:22:02,167 --> 01:22:03,001
So,

1112
01:22:04,586 --> 01:22:07,297
my whole daily life changed.

1113
01:22:08,882 --> 01:22:11,968
I can't go to the bar on the corner
to have a beer and relax,

1114
01:22:12,052 --> 01:22:14,095
or go out for a walk.

1115
01:22:16,181 --> 01:22:19,267
I can't go out at night
like I did my whole life.

1116
01:22:20,977 --> 01:22:24,481
So, I had to take a journey inward.

1117
01:22:59,140 --> 01:23:01,351
We went to record
the album in Sala Lavardén,

1118
01:23:01,434 --> 01:23:05,355
where I'd worked with Baglietto
in the early '80s.

1119
01:23:05,438 --> 01:23:07,732
We made it beautiful,
gorgeous, like a queen.

1120
01:23:07,816 --> 01:23:09,901
We carpeted it, removed the seats.

1121
01:23:09,985 --> 01:23:13,446
We set up an amazing control room.

1122
01:23:13,530 --> 01:23:15,615
We spent two unforgettable weeks there.

1123
01:23:21,496 --> 01:23:25,250
[young] What does every man do?
Go back to his home, his family.

1124
01:23:25,333 --> 01:23:29,587
See what happened there all these years.
See how I got here too.

1125
01:23:29,671 --> 01:23:33,174
I created an album about that.

1126
01:23:33,758 --> 01:23:37,595
How it works, from the perspective
of a guy who is now popular.

1127
01:23:37,679 --> 01:23:40,348
It makes for
a pretty interesting cocktail.

1128
01:23:40,432 --> 01:23:42,559
That's why it's called Circo Beat.

1129
01:24:12,672 --> 01:24:17,510
[Fito] This is a beautiful moment
because we just arrived in the States

1130
01:24:17,594 --> 01:24:19,971
to present and listen to <i>Novela</i>,

1131
01:24:20,472 --> 01:24:23,683
a project that started in 1988.

1132
01:24:24,851 --> 01:24:29,773
{\an8}I'm very happy,
and I hope everything is beautiful.

1133
01:24:29,856 --> 01:24:31,232
I'll keep you posted.

1134
01:24:31,733 --> 01:24:34,152
[majestic orchestral music playing]

1135
01:24:36,112 --> 01:24:38,615
[woman 1, in English]
Welcome to <i>A New York Evening</i>

1136
01:24:38,698 --> 01:24:41,326
with 11-time
Latin Grammy winner Fito Páez.

1137
01:24:43,078 --> 01:24:46,331
[woman 2 in Spanglish]
It'll be in Spanglish. We'll switch.

1138
01:24:46,414 --> 01:24:49,250
[in English]
I'm a Latin-American star, you know?

1139
01:24:49,334 --> 01:24:51,044
[audience laughing]

1140
01:24:51,127 --> 01:24:53,463
[orchestral music continues]

1141
01:24:53,546 --> 01:24:56,216
[woman 3, in Spanish]
We're celebrating this project.

1142
01:24:56,299 --> 01:24:58,802
[Fito] <i>Novela </i>was part
of every album I made.

1143
01:24:58,885 --> 01:25:01,679
It was part of the time I spent
in bed, space, music.

1144
01:25:01,763 --> 01:25:03,640
It was a member of my family.

1145
01:25:03,723 --> 01:25:07,435
[music ends with a piano coda]

1146
01:25:12,941 --> 01:25:15,110
[reporter] Where does Fito Páez write?

1147
01:25:16,111 --> 01:25:20,824
[Fito] Now I'm an empty-nester
because my kids left home.

1148
01:25:20,907 --> 01:25:23,326
I'm separated
from my girlfriend of ten years.

1149
01:25:23,409 --> 01:25:29,958
So, I'm kind of back to how I was before.
I write wherever the idea hits me.

1150
01:25:31,000 --> 01:25:33,962
Sometimes it's in hotels,

1151
01:25:34,045 --> 01:25:35,964
on planes…

1152
01:25:36,047 --> 01:25:41,594
I need a little bit of rest.

1153
01:25:42,137 --> 01:25:45,056
Like having a house

1154
01:25:45,140 --> 01:25:50,854
and prioritizing walks, the sun,

1155
01:25:50,937 --> 01:25:53,439
smaller things, you know?

1156
01:25:53,523 --> 01:25:55,400
And cooking too.

1157
01:26:05,660 --> 01:26:08,371
[Gus] Tiny Desk?
<i>I didn't know anything about it.</i>

1158
01:26:08,454 --> 01:26:10,039
<i>That format is amazing.</i>

1159
01:26:10,623 --> 01:26:13,126
[Fito] Listen, Gus.
Yeah, we're doing <i>Tiny Desk</i>.

1160
01:26:13,209 --> 01:26:16,379
I'll be in Washington on the 6th, 7th…

1161
01:26:16,462 --> 01:26:18,965
We'll rehearse the 7th and 8th.
Filming on the 9th.

1162
01:26:19,048 --> 01:26:21,426
I'll keep you posted. Big hug.

1163
01:26:24,220 --> 01:26:26,222
[suspenseful music playing]

1164
01:26:31,561 --> 01:26:35,231
I don't remember how many albums I made
before <i>Circo Beat</i>. A lot.

1165
01:26:35,315 --> 01:26:38,318
Then it stopped. It stopped.

1166
01:26:42,405 --> 01:26:45,366
<i>Circo Beat </i>was '94. <i>Abre </i>was '99.

1167
01:26:45,450 --> 01:26:51,080
Between <i>Circo Beat</i>
and <i>Abre</i>, I had no ideas.

1168
01:26:51,164 --> 01:26:53,208
But I was still trying anyway.

1169
01:26:53,291 --> 01:26:55,126
Ice. Frozen.

1170
01:26:55,210 --> 01:26:59,464
At one point, I got scared.
I thought, "What's going on here?"

1171
01:27:01,382 --> 01:27:03,927
I was really worried, man.

1172
01:27:04,010 --> 01:27:08,723
I mean, I was obviously doing things,
but I threw everything away.

1173
01:27:15,688 --> 01:27:19,025
Obviously, the first few
times those crises happen

1174
01:27:19,692 --> 01:27:22,904
they're scary.

1175
01:27:25,740 --> 01:27:29,619
One day, I suddenly wrote
"Al lado del camino."

1176
01:27:29,702 --> 01:27:32,288
The whole thing, almost like vomiting.

1177
01:27:32,372 --> 01:27:35,416
As if I had opened a faucet.

1178
01:27:35,500 --> 01:27:38,253
As if my solar plexus opened, right?

1179
01:27:41,756 --> 01:27:43,841
I was in bed with Ceci.

1180
01:27:43,925 --> 01:27:46,135
{\an8}The phone rang. André Midani asked…

1181
01:27:46,219 --> 01:27:48,429
{\an8}[on phone] <i>"Who do you want as producer?"</i>

1182
01:27:48,513 --> 01:27:49,681
{\an8}André loved joking.

1183
01:27:49,764 --> 01:27:53,434
I said, "Okay. Quincy Jones, Phil Ramone."

1184
01:27:53,518 --> 01:27:56,312
A week later, Phil calls me. He says,

1185
01:27:56,396 --> 01:27:59,232
[in English] "Hello, I'm Phil.
You're Fito Páez?"

1186
01:27:59,315 --> 01:28:01,693
[in Spanish] I nearly peed my pants.

1187
01:28:01,776 --> 01:28:04,404
He had produced
for Sinatra and Paul Simon.

1188
01:28:04,487 --> 01:28:06,239
He had done <i>Blood on the Tracks.</i>

1189
01:28:07,115 --> 01:28:10,201
{\an8}And then I met someone
who was absolutely relaxed

1190
01:28:10,285 --> 01:28:12,412
{\an8}and in control of all things music.

1191
01:28:12,495 --> 01:28:13,830
{\an8}RECORDING / CIRCO BEAT STUDIOS

1192
01:28:14,455 --> 01:28:17,333
[in English] When the studio
is alive and working,

1193
01:28:17,417 --> 01:28:20,586
the musicians come, the fire starts.

1194
01:28:20,670 --> 01:28:21,796
It's incredible.

1195
01:28:25,091 --> 01:28:26,926
[Fito, in Spanish] I was back.

1196
01:28:27,010 --> 01:28:29,762
Five years later. I came back strong.

1197
01:28:30,388 --> 01:28:34,976
<i>♪ I was a sad and enchanted boy ♪</i>

1198
01:28:35,059 --> 01:28:38,396
<i>♪ The Beatles, Legui rum, and wonders ♪</i>

1199
01:28:38,479 --> 01:28:42,358
<i>♪ Books, songs, and pianos ♪</i>

1200
01:28:42,442 --> 01:28:45,528
<i>♪ Movies, betrayals, and mysteries ♪</i>

1201
01:28:45,611 --> 01:28:46,654
<i>♪ My father ♪</i>

1202
01:28:46,738 --> 01:28:51,409
<i>♪ Beers, pills, mysteries, bad whiskey ♪</i>

1203
01:28:51,492 --> 01:28:53,911
<i>♪ Oil paintings, love, stages ♪</i>

1204
01:28:53,995 --> 01:28:57,790
<i>♪ Hunger, cold, crime</i>
<i>Money and my ten aunts ♪</i>

1205
01:28:57,874 --> 01:29:00,835
<i>♪ They made me this tangled man ♪</i>

1206
01:29:05,715 --> 01:29:09,302
[Fito, in English] Yes, with two eggs.
Breakfast meat…

1207
01:29:09,385 --> 01:29:12,638
Yes, but I don't understand
exactly what you say.

1208
01:29:12,722 --> 01:29:15,391
Ah, ham! Ham, ham…

1209
01:29:15,475 --> 01:29:16,934
Eggs. Scrambled, yes.

1210
01:29:17,018 --> 01:29:18,936
Toast and coffee, yes.

1211
01:29:19,020 --> 01:29:20,146
Uh, brown toast.

1212
01:29:20,229 --> 01:29:23,232
Thank you, my dear. You're so kind.

1213
01:29:23,316 --> 01:29:26,235
[in Spanish] With everything
you made me go through,

1214
01:29:26,319 --> 01:29:30,615
if I didn't have an episode,
it's because my system works well.

1215
01:29:30,698 --> 01:29:32,408
But you could have one, dude.

1216
01:29:32,492 --> 01:29:35,078
So, you could become a dangerous person.

1217
01:29:35,161 --> 01:29:36,913
For work.

1218
01:29:39,040 --> 01:29:40,958
It'd be more toward you.

1219
01:29:41,042 --> 01:29:43,503
-It would be more centralized.
-I'll be shielded.

1220
01:29:44,420 --> 01:29:47,757
Doesn't it reassure you
that I'm taking pills?

1221
01:29:47,840 --> 01:29:51,135
-Not really.
-Did I take one today or not?

1222
01:29:51,219 --> 01:29:52,845
See what I mean?

1223
01:29:52,929 --> 01:29:55,973
I think I did. I think I did.

1224
01:29:56,057 --> 01:29:58,893
I think you're going
to be working from home.

1225
01:30:00,978 --> 01:30:03,731
[waiter, in English] May I pour
coffee at the moment?

1226
01:30:03,815 --> 01:30:05,691
-You'd like coffee?
-[Carlos] No.

1227
01:30:05,775 --> 01:30:07,110
-[waiter] Got it?
-We do.

1228
01:30:07,193 --> 01:30:09,112
[waiter] Let me take that plastic.

1229
01:30:09,195 --> 01:30:11,197
[phone ringing]

1230
01:30:12,240 --> 01:30:16,077
[Fito, in Spanish] Well, at some point,
someone was going to call me

1231
01:30:16,160 --> 01:30:18,746
before my Berklee lecture, right?

1232
01:30:18,830 --> 01:30:20,498
<i>Yes, me. Here I am.</i>

1233
01:30:20,581 --> 01:30:22,458
Yes, but I called <i>you</i>, Margarita.

1234
01:30:22,542 --> 01:30:28,673
Remind my son, Diana,
that today his father will give a lecture

1235
01:30:28,756 --> 01:30:31,008
at Berklee University.

1236
01:30:31,092 --> 01:30:37,014
And I haven't received any calls.
I didn't get any flowers.

1237
01:30:37,098 --> 01:30:40,143
<i>The dollar is pricey.</i>

1238
01:30:40,226 --> 01:30:45,314
<i>Come on, Dad.</i>
<i>You just asked, "Who's calling me?"</i>

1239
01:30:45,398 --> 01:30:49,318
<i>-I'm calling you.</i>
-No, Margarita. I called you.

1240
01:30:49,402 --> 01:30:53,030
[Margarita scoffs]
<i>Okay, but I asked how you were doing.</i>

1241
01:30:53,114 --> 01:30:56,993
"I asked how you were doing"
is the same as "I'm calling you."

1242
01:30:57,076 --> 01:30:58,327
-It's the same.
<i>-Yes.</i>

1243
01:30:58,411 --> 01:31:02,331
-[Martín] <i>Congratulations.</i>
-No, to cheer me on.

1244
01:31:02,832 --> 01:31:05,751
<i>Yeah. We're with you.</i>

1245
01:31:06,377 --> 01:31:07,962
[woman laughs]

1246
01:31:08,045 --> 01:31:11,591
You and my daughter are the worst.

1247
01:31:11,674 --> 01:31:14,093
You're two rascals.

1248
01:31:14,177 --> 01:31:15,553
This is neglect.

1249
01:31:15,636 --> 01:31:19,474
<i>♪ Maldivina is blonder than Marilyn ♪</i>

1250
01:31:19,974 --> 01:31:24,061
<i>♪ Turbialuz is wearing black by Ramírez ♪</i>

1251
01:31:24,687 --> 01:31:28,357
Listen, I feel neglected today.

1252
01:31:28,441 --> 01:31:29,775
<i>Oh, God!</i>

1253
01:31:29,859 --> 01:31:32,278
-[laughs]
-I love you. [kisses]

1254
01:31:32,904 --> 01:31:34,489
<i>Love you. Bye.</i>

1255
01:31:55,426 --> 01:31:56,886
[Fito] Two, three…

1256
01:31:57,845 --> 01:32:00,348
<i>♪ La, la, la, la, la ♪</i>

1257
01:32:02,350 --> 01:32:05,061
<i>♪ La, la, la, la, la ♪</i>

1258
01:32:06,771 --> 01:32:10,107
{\an8}<i>♪ La, le, li, le, li, le, le ♪</i>

1259
01:32:10,191 --> 01:32:12,902
<i>♪ La, li, li, le, la, la ♪</i>

1260
01:32:14,487 --> 01:32:17,782
<i>♪ La, li, li, le, la, la ♪</i>

1261
01:32:17,865 --> 01:32:19,909
[cheering]

1262
01:32:19,992 --> 01:32:21,661
Um…

1263
01:32:24,080 --> 01:32:27,166
-It's not a rock concert today.
-[group laughs]

1264
01:32:27,250 --> 01:32:30,253
[in English] It's not
a rock-and-roll concert today.

1265
01:32:30,336 --> 01:32:36,842
It's an extract of an essay about music
in the 21st century.

1266
01:32:37,552 --> 01:32:42,014
[in Spanish] So,
"Music in Times of Mass Dementia."

1267
01:32:42,932 --> 01:32:47,603
Today, the world music seems
to be on its deathbed.

1268
01:32:48,688 --> 01:32:54,610
The lack of passion
in today's music is overwhelming.

1269
01:32:55,111 --> 01:33:00,241
This territory has been conquered
by the owners of the world

1270
01:33:00,324 --> 01:33:05,037
in search of
the annihilation of specificities,

1271
01:33:05,121 --> 01:33:10,126
shattering with the greatest amount
of force

1272
01:33:10,209 --> 01:33:14,171
the lightning
of musical language standardization

1273
01:33:14,255 --> 01:33:17,592
in all its imaginable forms."

1274
01:33:18,467 --> 01:33:22,054
What do you want? More money? More land?

1275
01:33:22,138 --> 01:33:25,600
More shares, more cars, more yachts?

1276
01:33:25,683 --> 01:33:28,477
What are you going to do
with all that, man?

1277
01:33:28,561 --> 01:33:30,563
It's simple.

1278
01:33:30,646 --> 01:33:37,278
You have to share, make the audience cry.
Make them sing together.

1279
01:33:37,361 --> 01:33:40,489
We feel good when we're all together,

1280
01:33:40,573 --> 01:33:43,868
because together we'll go
to an unknown place.

1281
01:33:44,827 --> 01:33:46,203
"Music is positive."

1282
01:33:47,121 --> 01:33:51,208
"It helps, accompanies,
teaches, heals, and transforms."

1283
01:33:52,585 --> 01:33:56,547
"Music as a weapon
against all the evils of this world."

1284
01:33:58,257 --> 01:34:00,343
Something I never thought I'd write,

1285
01:34:00,426 --> 01:34:04,055
but that I thought was appropriate
for wrapping up with,

1286
01:34:04,138 --> 01:34:06,349
is the "Manual for Today's Youth."

1287
01:34:06,432 --> 01:34:08,684
[group chuckles]

1288
01:34:08,768 --> 01:34:11,395
"Listen in silence
to music you're not familiar with,

1289
01:34:11,479 --> 01:34:15,232
for one hour,
turning off any distracting devices."

1290
01:34:17,401 --> 01:34:21,614
"If you don't have kids,
throw everything away, and do only this."

1291
01:34:23,574 --> 01:34:26,369
"Make love to the music
you like the most."

1292
01:34:26,869 --> 01:34:30,498
"Dance to Argentine and Colombian <i>cumbia</i>.
Learn to see the differences."

1293
01:34:30,581 --> 01:34:33,042
[students laughing]

1294
01:34:33,125 --> 01:34:36,379
"Dance to Prince and Babasónicos.
Learn to see the differences."

1295
01:34:36,462 --> 01:34:38,214
{\an8}LIBRARY OF CONGRESS
WASHINGTON, D.C.

1296
01:34:38,297 --> 01:34:42,802
"Learn from the masters.
Masters are not just musicians."

1297
01:34:43,803 --> 01:34:46,389
"Anyone can teach you
something about life."

1298
01:34:48,557 --> 01:34:53,938
-Ah! No! No!
-[in English] Yeah. That's Mozart.

1299
01:34:55,189 --> 01:34:58,234
And no mistakes. No correcting. Ink.

1300
01:34:58,317 --> 01:35:03,114
-There's nothing.
-[Fito] It's like you're touching God. No?

1301
01:35:04,156 --> 01:35:07,952
If God exists, it's in Mozart.

1302
01:35:08,035 --> 01:35:11,455
[in Spanish] "Getting frustrated
is the best thing that can happen."

1303
01:35:11,539 --> 01:35:14,834
"When you find your voice,
you won't want to go back to shore."

1304
01:35:14,917 --> 01:35:18,295
"Just play music
and drink with your friends."

1305
01:35:18,379 --> 01:35:22,842
"Music has taught you this whole time
that you still have a long way to go,

1306
01:35:22,925 --> 01:35:25,720
and that in the time you have left
to live,

1307
01:35:25,803 --> 01:35:29,432
you'll be comforted by the love
of all the good and honest things

1308
01:35:29,515 --> 01:35:31,392
you've given to others."

1309
01:35:31,475 --> 01:35:33,769
[group whistles and cheers]

1310
01:35:33,853 --> 01:35:36,272
[upbeat music playing]

1311
01:35:45,573 --> 01:35:49,785
[Fito] Thank you all.
You're wonderful musicians.

1312
01:35:55,332 --> 01:35:56,667
Let's focus.

1313
01:35:58,335 --> 01:36:00,546
-This.
-I don't know if I'll go to dinner.

1314
01:36:00,629 --> 01:36:04,008
-[man] Rest your voice.
-[woman] No, go rest.

1315
01:36:13,267 --> 01:36:15,269
[suspenseful music playing]

1316
01:36:33,996 --> 01:36:37,249
[Fito coughs]

1317
01:36:40,961 --> 01:36:42,755
[continues coughing]

1318
01:36:42,838 --> 01:36:45,466
[reporter speaking indistinctly on news]

1319
01:36:52,890 --> 01:36:56,018
[reporter 1] <i>That's right, Valentina.</i>
<i>There's a clear trend.</i>

1320
01:36:56,101 --> 01:36:58,854
<i>If you're Argentine</i>
<i>and want to go on vacation,</i>

1321
01:36:58,938 --> 01:37:01,941
<i>you'll find that most people tell us</i>

1322
01:37:02,024 --> 01:37:06,529
<i>they choose Brazil or Chile for shopping.</i>

1323
01:37:06,612 --> 01:37:10,199
<i>But people coming</i>
<i>from abroad to Argentina…</i>

1324
01:37:10,282 --> 01:37:13,035
["139 Lexatins" playing]

1325
01:37:21,794 --> 01:37:23,712
I had split up with Cecilia.

1326
01:37:23,796 --> 01:37:26,382
Cecilia had split up
with me, to be more precise.

1327
01:37:26,465 --> 01:37:28,133
And I was very depressed.

1328
01:37:32,972 --> 01:37:35,933
-Is this Chacras de Hummus?
-Yeah.

1329
01:37:36,016 --> 01:37:39,812
[Fito] Coki and Carlos,
my two best friends in life.

1330
01:37:40,813 --> 01:37:43,232
Always by my side, you know? Nearby.

1331
01:37:43,315 --> 01:37:46,110
Raising my kids and making music.

1332
01:37:47,778 --> 01:37:52,116
{\an8}We're presenting Martín's album.
His first album, called Palangana.

1333
01:37:52,199 --> 01:37:54,410
We made a beautiful pact once.

1334
01:37:55,244 --> 01:38:00,082
If any of us falls at any point,
the other two will destroy him.

1335
01:38:00,165 --> 01:38:01,584
[Coki] Pure Brazil.

1336
01:38:02,376 --> 01:38:05,212
[Fito] What are you? The rhythm duo?

1337
01:38:05,296 --> 01:38:09,258
And so, these two would give me
a very hard time, right?

1338
01:38:09,341 --> 01:38:12,344
"Oh, look.
The gentleman is depressed," they'd say.

1339
01:38:12,428 --> 01:38:15,347
["Volver a mí" playing]

1340
01:38:15,431 --> 01:38:19,852
It was a gesture of love and encouragement
because it made us laugh.

1341
01:38:19,935 --> 01:38:21,729
It always made us laugh.

1342
01:38:21,812 --> 01:38:23,522
Guys, I'm here!

1343
01:38:23,606 --> 01:38:25,774
Coki! I'm so glad you came.

1344
01:38:25,858 --> 01:38:28,569
-Why are you so late?
-We've been waiting for two days.

1345
01:38:29,361 --> 01:38:36,368
[Romina] I met Rodolfo one night
at a bar, around three in the morning.

1346
01:38:36,452 --> 01:38:38,662
[Fito] In this magical place.

1347
01:38:38,746 --> 01:38:43,000
[Romina] He said, "I know
what you're looking for. A smart man."

1348
01:38:43,083 --> 01:38:45,210
He said, "Here. That's me."

1349
01:38:45,294 --> 01:38:48,505
And then we kept going until, well,

1350
01:38:49,590 --> 01:38:50,841
we had…

1351
01:38:58,265 --> 01:39:02,227
{\an8}They were fighting,
so he got another girlfriend.

1352
01:39:02,311 --> 01:39:04,647
{\an8}Then he left her, and that's how it went.

1353
01:39:04,730 --> 01:39:05,564
{\an8}DAUGHTER

1354
01:39:08,275 --> 01:39:10,986
{\an8}I wouldn't trade the passion of my love
for my kids.

1355
01:39:11,070 --> 01:39:13,697
Not for music,
not for literature, nothing.

1356
01:39:14,990 --> 01:39:18,535
And now you think, "Fuck.
Why didn't I have them before?"

1357
01:39:19,870 --> 01:39:21,747
["Margarita" playing]

1358
01:39:21,830 --> 01:39:25,960
<i>♪ You make me happy ♪</i>

1359
01:39:26,043 --> 01:39:30,506
<i>♪ When you wake up ♪</i>

1360
01:39:30,589 --> 01:39:35,010
<i>♪ You make me happy ♪</i>

1361
01:39:35,094 --> 01:39:39,515
<i>♪ You make the world shine ♪</i>

1362
01:39:39,598 --> 01:39:44,311
<i>♪ I want to go ♪</i>

1363
01:39:44,395 --> 01:39:47,898
<i>♪ To the moon with you ♪</i>

1364
01:39:48,524 --> 01:39:52,653
<i>♪ You make me happy ♪</i>

1365
01:39:52,736 --> 01:39:56,907
<i>♪ You make me feel fleeting ♪</i>

1366
01:39:56,991 --> 01:40:01,745
<i>♪ I want to go ♪</i>

1367
01:40:01,829 --> 01:40:06,291
<i>♪ To the moon with you ♪</i>

1368
01:40:06,375 --> 01:40:07,626
<i>♪ Ah! ♪</i>

1369
01:40:07,710 --> 01:40:11,672
<i>♪ Long live the world and life ♪</i>

1370
01:40:11,755 --> 01:40:16,385
<i>♪ Long live voices and excitement ♪</i>

1371
01:40:16,468 --> 01:40:20,597
<i>♪ This is what I want</i>
<i>To leave you, Margarita ♪</i>

1372
01:40:20,681 --> 01:40:27,688
<i>♪ A world that's</i>
<i>A bit more Rolling Stone ♪</i>

1373
01:40:30,107 --> 01:40:31,692
[coughs] Coming.

1374
01:40:36,655 --> 01:40:38,407
Dear Roque, look.

1375
01:40:38,490 --> 01:40:43,620
I woke up today, first 37,
then 38.3, then 38.7.

1376
01:40:44,705 --> 01:40:46,582
That was the last one.

1377
01:40:47,332 --> 01:40:51,295
I have a high fever
half an hour before the concert.

1378
01:40:54,506 --> 01:40:57,301
[rehearses a cheerful melody]

1379
01:40:58,010 --> 01:40:59,762
[tuba melody on keyboard]

1380
01:41:00,679 --> 01:41:02,473
{\an8}[saxophonist tuning]

1381
01:41:02,556 --> 01:41:06,810
{\an8}[in English] Thank you.
Uh, Carlos, your acoustic guitar, please.

1382
01:41:07,644 --> 01:41:10,272
[in Spanish] It's four songs.
I do have some energy.

1383
01:41:10,355 --> 01:41:12,900
[drummer playing beat]

1384
01:41:18,072 --> 01:41:19,281
[Fito huffs]

1385
01:41:25,370 --> 01:41:26,663
Ugh…

1386
01:41:29,458 --> 01:41:31,794
It's burning.

1387
01:41:32,711 --> 01:41:35,714
-My head. A lot.
-[woman] Your head?

1388
01:41:37,591 --> 01:41:39,927
-We can always cancel.
-No.

1389
01:41:42,179 --> 01:41:44,348
-But you can…
-The day after tomorrow, yes.

1390
01:41:51,146 --> 01:41:52,397
Want another one now?

1391
01:42:01,240 --> 01:42:04,243
[emotional piano music playing]

1392
01:42:30,519 --> 01:42:32,896
[speaking inaudibly]

1393
01:42:36,275 --> 01:42:38,318
[woman clears throat]

1394
01:42:39,862 --> 01:42:45,576
[Fito] I'll be like Maradona
with the fatal sprain.

1395
01:42:45,659 --> 01:42:48,120
It could go well or it could go wrong.

1396
01:42:48,787 --> 01:42:50,622
I'll try to make it go well.

1397
01:42:51,498 --> 01:42:55,836
If it goes wrong,
you guys will be to blame.

1398
01:42:55,919 --> 01:42:57,379
-Of course.
-Yeah.

1399
01:43:00,799 --> 01:43:02,593
[man, in English] Okay, everybody.

1400
01:43:04,970 --> 01:43:06,972
We've had a lot of bands here,

1401
01:43:07,055 --> 01:43:09,391
but very few times
have we had an actual legend.

1402
01:43:09,474 --> 01:43:12,603
It's extremely…
It's such a big deal for us

1403
01:43:12,686 --> 01:43:15,647
to have a musician who defies genre,

1404
01:43:15,731 --> 01:43:18,275
who defies country, who defies everything.

1405
01:43:18,358 --> 01:43:21,028
World… known world-wide.

1406
01:43:21,778 --> 01:43:23,030
[in Spanish] That's Fito.

1407
01:43:23,113 --> 01:43:25,240
[in English] So, please,
welcome Fito Páez.

1408
01:43:26,617 --> 01:43:29,578
[Fito, in Spanish]
Thanks to NPR for inviting us.

1409
01:43:29,661 --> 01:43:32,206
And <i>Tiny Desk</i>
for giving us this beautiful moment.

1410
01:43:32,289 --> 01:43:33,916
It's live worldwide.

1411
01:43:33,999 --> 01:43:35,667
"Sale el sol."

1412
01:43:35,751 --> 01:43:37,794
Right? From <i>Novela.</i>

1413
01:43:37,878 --> 01:43:40,172
[playing "Sale el sol"]

1414
01:43:46,136 --> 01:43:47,512
One, two, three.

1415
01:44:45,821 --> 01:44:48,407
Thank you all for helping us make music.

1416
01:44:48,490 --> 01:44:51,410
Because music doesn't exist
without each other.

1417
01:44:51,493 --> 01:44:53,287
Besides, you cured my fever.

1418
01:44:53,370 --> 01:44:54,788
I love you.

1419
01:44:56,832 --> 01:44:58,542
-The sun rises!
-[woman] Come on!

1420
01:45:16,685 --> 01:45:18,437
[Fito] What do we need?

1421
01:45:18,520 --> 01:45:20,314
Well, what do we need?

1422
01:45:20,397 --> 01:45:23,108
I would need a lot of things.

1423
01:45:23,191 --> 01:45:25,402
For example, to be loved.

1424
01:45:25,485 --> 01:45:26,820
But you are already loved.

1425
01:45:26,903 --> 01:45:29,364
Well, but not in the way I want, Matías.

1426
01:45:29,448 --> 01:45:32,743
What do you…? In what way?
How would you like to be…?

1427
01:45:32,826 --> 01:45:37,414
Well, loved like the child I was.

1428
01:45:37,497 --> 01:45:40,834
-Oh, okay.
-But you're now 62 years old.

1429
01:45:40,917 --> 01:45:42,711
-How many?
-62?

1430
01:45:42,794 --> 01:45:44,254
No, not to me.

1431
01:45:45,672 --> 01:45:51,428
I feel totally like a child. Ageless.

1432
01:45:57,851 --> 01:46:00,604
[dramatic music]

1433
01:46:00,687 --> 01:46:04,149
[Fito speaks indistinctly]

1434
01:46:13,200 --> 01:46:15,369
[music grows chaotic]

1435
01:46:18,246 --> 01:46:20,123
[music peaks, fades]

1436
01:46:27,130 --> 01:46:31,343
[Fito] There's a photo
in which I'm barely 2 years old.

1437
01:46:32,010 --> 01:46:35,972
I'm sitting on the terrace
of the house on Balcarce Street.

1438
01:46:36,681 --> 01:46:39,434
Surrounded by toys. Dozens of them.

1439
01:46:40,060 --> 01:46:42,104
And yet, I'm crying.

1440
01:46:43,647 --> 01:46:46,024
All the women in my life and I

1441
01:46:46,566 --> 01:46:50,946
had to coexist with that boy
and his dead mother.

1442
01:46:54,241 --> 01:46:56,243
[emotional music playing]

1443
01:46:57,702 --> 01:47:01,081
My mom died because
her placenta was removed incorrectly.

1444
01:47:01,164 --> 01:47:03,708
So, well, it's my fault.

1445
01:47:03,792 --> 01:47:06,670
So, I created a character

1446
01:47:06,753 --> 01:47:09,673
with which, after going overboard,
I'd tell her,

1447
01:47:09,756 --> 01:47:13,885
"Mom, don't worry. I'm coming."

1448
01:47:13,969 --> 01:47:15,220
"I'll die soon."

1449
01:47:16,221 --> 01:47:20,475
So now, after the fall,
I did feel my mother's protection.

1450
01:47:20,559 --> 01:47:23,520
I did feel my father's
and my grandmothers' protection.

1451
01:47:24,563 --> 01:47:26,481
I did feel protected by the dead.

1452
01:47:26,565 --> 01:47:29,651
Because I didn't hurt my hip or my head.

1453
01:47:31,653 --> 01:47:35,782
And they said,
"You're going to stay here."

1454
01:47:35,866 --> 01:47:37,742
"We'll help you."

1455
01:47:39,077 --> 01:47:41,413
"And you'll go through physical pain."

1456
01:47:41,913 --> 01:47:42,956
First.

1457
01:47:43,748 --> 01:47:46,376
-I didn't know the other one was coming.
-Yes.

1458
01:47:48,170 --> 01:47:52,007
"If you feel pain, you're alive.
We don't feel pain because we're dead."

1459
01:47:52,090 --> 01:47:54,509
"You're alive. We're dead. Stop whining."

1460
01:47:54,593 --> 01:47:56,344
I'd written it in <i>La puta diabla.</i>

1461
01:47:56,428 --> 01:48:00,140
-You hadn't been able to let it go.
-Now I get it.

1462
01:48:29,127 --> 01:48:32,297
Oh, the artist has arrived.

1463
01:48:33,173 --> 01:48:35,091
Oh, my God.

1464
01:48:35,175 --> 01:48:36,635
{\an8}DAUGHTER

1465
01:48:36,718 --> 01:48:37,719
{\an8}Hi, Mati.

1466
01:48:39,763 --> 01:48:43,892
[Fito] As you get older,
you feel the need to be lighter.

1467
01:48:44,893 --> 01:48:47,187
Especially after the accident.

1468
01:48:51,483 --> 01:48:53,068
[Margarita] What's this?

1469
01:48:53,151 --> 01:49:00,158
[Fito] I realized I needed
to focus on the fundamental issues.

1470
01:49:01,159 --> 01:49:05,956
On fiercely protecting family
and real friendships.

1471
01:49:06,706 --> 01:49:11,044
And also on taking care
of the core of my madness.

1472
01:49:11,127 --> 01:49:16,299
That chaos and order
in which everything you do is managed

1473
01:49:16,383 --> 01:49:18,343
should be protected.

1474
01:49:30,146 --> 01:49:32,649
Finally, opening night.

1475
01:49:32,732 --> 01:49:33,858
[in English] I'm back.

1476
01:49:36,987 --> 01:49:41,825
["Cavalleria rusticana: Intermezzo"
playing]

1477
01:49:45,453 --> 01:49:47,455
[cinematic tune continues]

1478
01:50:24,492 --> 01:50:27,787
-[classical music continues]
-[singing inaudibly]

1479
01:50:57,484 --> 01:51:00,904
-[song fades]
-["El mundo cabe en una canción" playing]

1480
01:51:00,987 --> 01:51:04,157
WE DEDICATE THIS FILM
TO OUR BELOVED FAMILIES,

1481
01:51:04,240 --> 01:51:07,911
TO OUR DEAR FRIENDS,
AND TO THE GREAT ADOLFO ARISTARAIN

1482
01:51:10,288 --> 01:51:12,874
<i>♪ The roses of my first love ♪</i>

1483
01:51:12,957 --> 01:51:15,418
<i>♪ My parents, the first song ♪</i>

1484
01:51:15,502 --> 01:51:19,923
<i>♪ The first kiss, the first heartache ♪</i>

1485
01:51:20,006 --> 01:51:22,801
<i>♪ The man I didn't want to be ♪</i>

1486
01:51:22,884 --> 01:51:25,845
<i>♪ The August Förster</i>
<i>From which I set sail ♪</i>

1487
01:51:25,929 --> 01:51:30,558
<i>♪ To distant countries ♪</i>

1488
01:51:31,101 --> 01:51:35,939
<i>♪ And something was already</i>
<i>There almost discovering us ♪</i>

1489
01:51:36,022 --> 01:51:41,361
<i>♪ It was already breathing by my side ♪</i>

1490
01:51:41,444 --> 01:51:43,780
<i>♪ And this is what I learned ♪</i>

1491
01:51:43,863 --> 01:51:46,658
<i>♪ And today I come to tell you ♪</i>

1492
01:51:46,741 --> 01:51:51,663
<i>♪ The world fits within a song ♪</i>

1493
01:51:51,746 --> 01:51:56,710
<i>♪ All music speaks to me ♪</i>

1494
01:51:56,793 --> 01:52:03,633
<i>♪ All things connect in my heart ♪</i>

1495
01:52:04,384 --> 01:52:06,678
<i>♪ Ah! ♪</i>

1496
01:52:06,761 --> 01:52:09,180
<i>♪ I understand you well ♪</i>

1497
01:52:09,264 --> 01:52:14,561
<i>♪ You know I understand you well ♪</i>

1498
01:52:14,644 --> 01:52:19,774
<i>♪ And even if it's all a sham ♪</i>

1499
01:52:19,858 --> 01:52:24,404
<i>♪ I know the whole world fits ♪</i>

1500
01:52:24,487 --> 01:52:29,617
<i>♪ Within a song ♪</i>

1501
01:52:29,701 --> 01:52:34,038
Subtitle translation by: María C. Delgado